{
    "bug_id": 1515,
    "summary": "for dotted borders, want round dots instead of square dots",
    "comments": [
        {
            "text": "The CSS specification do not detail how dotted borders should be drawn. Opera\n3.50 draws them as \"zebra crossings\" when the border width increases. I think\nthat is a better solution than nglayout's current \"square dots\" policy. Section\n(5) of the test page lets you test this.",
            "creator": "howcome@w3.org",
            "timestamp": "1998-11-23T18:59:54Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "How about actual round dots for dotted borders?",
            "creator": "peterl-retired@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-21T00:27:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Now that would be cool.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-17T16:53:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Moving to M11. Not an M10 blocker.",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-24T21:45:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This won't be addressed in the near future, maybee in the next version... or if\nthere is time...",
            "creator": "dcone@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-11T14:25:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Verified  REMIND",
            "creator": "christinehoff4@aol.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-11T20:10:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Migrating from {css1} to css1 keyword. The {css1}, {css2}, {css3} and {css-moz}\nradars should now be considered deprecated in favour of keywords.\nI am *really* sorry about the spam...",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-14T00:00:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This has a target of M11. That was some time ago.",
            "creator": "webmaster@richinstyle.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-03-10T14:20:47Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Reopening and moving to future...",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-07T21:01:09Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Nominating nsbeta2,nsbeta3,rtm. Recc. nsbeta2+, falling through to nsbeta3+ and \nrtm+ if necessary. This is a W3C CSS1 Official Test Suite compliance bug.",
            "creator": "ekrock@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-15T02:10:38Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Sorry, to clarify: this is not an nsbeta2 stopper, but I'd permit checking in \nprior to nsbeta2 if we have a fix. Recc. nsbeta2+[some lenient date-], falling \nthrough to nsbeta3+ hard stop if not fixed during nsbeta2. This is a W3C CSS1 \nOfficial Test Suite compliance bug.",
            "creator": "ekrock@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-15T02:40:31Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Eric: This is possibly one of the bugs I would delay the most to fix, and is\nIMHO not even remotely important for FCS. There is no _bug_ here, we are merely\nsuggested that the dots of 'dotted' borders be drawn as round dots instead of\nsquare ones.\n\nAre you sure this should be nsbeta3 hardstop?\n",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-15T10:31:12Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Putting on [nsbeta2-] radar. Not critical to beta2. ",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-15T20:58:22Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Sorry, and thanks Ian! I totally misunderstood this report; I thought we were \n*failing* the test. Ian's correct; there's not bug & this goes to future.\n\nClarifying Summary from \"dotted borders\" to \"for dotted borders, want round dots \ninstead of square dots.\" \n\nTo simplify tracking & searching (in particular, to avoid wasting the time of \npeople who will be reviewing the [nsbeta2-] bugs for nsbeta3, etc., I'm taking \nthe unusual (but in this case reasonable) step of removing all of the \nPDT-related markings from both Keywords and Status Whiteboard. (It was only my \ngoof that put them there in the first place.) Removing \"nsbeta2,nsbeta3,rtm\" \nfrom Keywords and \"[nsbeta2-]\" from Status Whiteboard.\n\nAlso Ian, should this really have the \"css1\" keyword? It's not a compliance \nissue. Your call.",
            "creator": "ekrock@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-16T00:28:17Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Eric: I'm using the 'css1' keyword for anything CSS1-related, not just for \ncompliance issues. Almost all the bugs marked CSS1 which are currently Future,\nfor example, are not actually compliance issues.\n",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-16T13:55:16Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Internet Explorer 5.5 already does this, for the record :-)",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-25T21:47:26Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I am marking this as invalid for a few reasons.  \n1.)  Currently all our border code goes thru routines that are set up to draw \nrectangular type dots, so dashes and dotted use the same code as well as all \nborder styles.  To change this would be a pretty big job.. not worth the errors \nthat might be introduced.\n2.) Using dots could complicate some issues like going around corners.\n3.) Looking at examples in books.. the way we implemented dotted and dashed seem \nconsistent with how they handle this issue.  \n4.) This is so low a priority that even if we did decide to do it.. it would \nprobably never be addressed..",
            "creator": "dcone@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-08T15:25:31Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Reopening. INVALID is the wrong resolution, it should be WONTFIX if we actually\ndid decide to close it unfixed.\n\nHowever the competition successfully implements this -- and so it is a valid \nenhancement request even if it is not a high priority one.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-09T10:46:12Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Moving off dcone's plate and onto the helpwanted pile...",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-09T10:47:08Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Netscape's standard compliance QA team reorganised itself once again, so taking \nremaining non-tables style bugs. Sorry about the spam. I tried to get this done \ndirectly at the database level, but apparently that is \"not easy because of the \nshadow db\", \"plus it screws up the audit trail\", so no can do...",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2001-02-13T00:28:27Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Dotted borders have square dots on Mac, too. Changing to All/All.",
            "creator": "bugmail@impromptu.at",
            "timestamp": "2002-07-11T06:34:12Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Taking (and ->P2), although I may not get to it anytime soon.",
            "creator": "dbaron@dbaron.org",
            "timestamp": "2002-12-22T03:16:46Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Just an FYI: The Oxford American Dictionary says a dot is \"a small, *round* point\".\n\nBut, in my opinion, what are the advantages to having round dots for dotted\nborders anyway?  A dotted border is under most, if not all, circumstances, so\nsmall that you can't tell if it is a round dot or a rectangular dot anyway.",
            "creator": "brant@gurganus.name",
            "timestamp": "2003-01-26T16:38:43Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "In most cases, yes, but not all.  If you're into CSS semi-hacks, it's possible\nin IE5/Mac to do this:\n\n   <span style=\"border-left: 1em dotted purple;\">blah</span>\n\n...and get a single purple circle to the left of the content of the element. \nFor that matter, defining a truly huge single-dot border can give you non-image,\ncompletely text-zoom-scaling circles.\n\nGranted, it's an edge case, but it's still something Gecko still can't do.  And\neven in less extreme cases, an author might want a 5px wide border with round\ndots in it instead of smaller dashes.  So we really should, at some point, get\nour dots to be actual dots.  I wouldn't call it a top priority or anything but\nit would certainly be nice.",
            "creator": "emeyer@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2003-01-27T14:52:39Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Every definition I can find for \"dot\" says dots are round, and I agree -\nbesides, it looks nicer.",
            "creator": "mozilla@gkn.me.uk",
            "timestamp": "2003-10-31T23:59:14Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Taking for preliminary investigation.  biesi suggests looking for this code in nsCSSRendering.cpp.",
            "creator": "pkasting@google.com",
            "timestamp": "2006-06-08T17:51:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "See also bug 19963, which requires more significant changes to dotted and dashed borders, and is actually somewhat necessary for colors with alpha, which we now support with cairo builds.",
            "creator": "dbaron@dbaron.org",
            "timestamp": "2006-06-08T18:11:38Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Note that one of the interesting questions is what to do at the corners when dotted borders have different widths.  I suggest an ellipse, since that allows the two sides of the diagonal to be drawn independently, which does the right thing in the normal case (same width) and does something at least somewhat reasonable in cases where one side is dotted an the other is not.  But you might want to test what other browsers do there as well (but consider their solutions in light of how well they work for bug 19963 and how well they work for rgba colors).",
            "creator": "dbaron@dbaron.org",
            "timestamp": "2006-06-08T18:16:21Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This would be a nice nhancement, but I'm not going to get it in the foreseeable future, so I'm going to go ahead and dump it to nobody.",
            "creator": "pkasting@google.com",
            "timestamp": "2006-06-22T00:25:48Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "A proposal for the end result of dots and bevels has been attached to bug 19963.",
            "creator": "pamg.bugs@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2006-07-11T22:39:26Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Fixed by thebes rendering rewrite; dots are used for dotted borders > 2px in size.",
            "creator": "vladimir@pobox.com",
            "timestamp": "2007-05-01T05:16:15Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Created attachment 263852\nscreenshot of the new round-dotted border\n\nMaybe it is just me, but personally I would not call this fixed.\nThe circles are cut off at the corners.",
            "creator": "stefan.mayrhofer@openmailbox.org",
            "timestamp": "2007-05-05T12:15:44Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Fixing that is bug 379303 -- should be straightforward, just haven't written the code yet.",
            "creator": "vladimir@pobox.com",
            "timestamp": "2007-05-07T19:59:58Z"
        }
    ]
}

{
    "bug_id": 2652,
    "summary": "would like mailbox:// command to work in Navigator window.",
    "comments": [
        {
            "text": "<Moved enhancement from bugsplat 80213. Relevant contents are pasted here.\nSorry if this is misassigned.  Taking a first stab at these new components.>\n\nYou used to be able to call the mailbox from a Navigator window by using\nthe command \"mailbox://\" but this doesn't work in Communicator anymore.\n\nThis needs to call up the Messaging Window - can this be put in....\n\nthanks\n\njones@netscape.com x4573",
            "creator": "lchiang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-01-26T23:04:45Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This sounds badly broken. M6 to make sure URL dispatching is right in 5.0",
            "creator": "phil@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-28T01:00:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Phil, we won't have any url dispatching from the browser until Necko is done.\nI'm pushing this out to M7 if not later as I don't expect this functionality\nfrom necko by M6.",
            "creator": "mscott@mozilla.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-28T01:02:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I'm surprised that deciding which window to run a URL in is covered by necko.\nWarren, is it?",
            "creator": "phil@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-28T16:58:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This is the url dispatching stuff. The url would get loaded from the url bar\ninto netlib which looks at the protocol scheme and kicks the url out to a\nregistered protocol handler for that scheme. At least that was my take on this\nbug...",
            "creator": "mscott@mozilla.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-28T17:09:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "It sounds like the current netlib isn't doing this. It works in necko...!",
            "creator": "warrensomebody@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-28T17:44:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "But but but...in 4.x there's a bunch of code in each FE to look at each URL\nbefore giving it to netlib. The FE decides (with help from the libmsg backend)\nwhat kind of window to run the URL in. It's this code which brings up a mail\nwindow instead of allowing the mailbox:// URL to run in the browser window.\n\nIs this kind of thing really necko's responsibility?",
            "creator": "phil@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-28T21:39:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Ok, I misunderstood. Necko's responsibility is to find the protocol handler for\nthe URL, and get the data back to the application. It's the application's\nresponsibility to provide a context for loading the URL -- one that is wired up\nto talk to the right application window. I don't have a work item for that on\nthe schedule (although maybe I should).",
            "creator": "warrensomebody@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-29T19:06:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Yes, it seems like we should define some interface between necko and the\napplication which knows what type of window is required for a given URL.",
            "creator": "phil@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-29T23:22:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I don't think it's so much an interface that knows what type of window is\nrequired, but some implementations of stream handlers that upon receiving the\ndata direct it to the appropriate application window. The app would choose the\nappropriate stream handler as part of making the url request.\n\nI've added a week to our schedule to help with this task.",
            "creator": "warrensomebody@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-30T01:17:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "We're not going to be there in M7 for this bug pushing it down the pipe until\npost necko integration.....",
            "creator": "mscott@mozilla.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-04T07:04:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Moving all Mail/News Networking bugs to Mail/News Networking-Mail\n\nThis may re-open previously Verified bugs due to a Bugzilla bug...if so, I will\nfix those bugs.",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-20T19:00:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I still have some issues about protocol registratoin that need worked out with\nthe necko team before this can become a reality. In particular, I can't support\nthe nsIChannel interface in its current form for pluggable protocols like\nmailbox because they aren't stream oriented.\n\nWarren and I have talked briefly about pulling out the stream specific interface\nmethods in nsIChannel. I'll follow up on this post necko landing.",
            "creator": "mscott@mozilla.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-25T22:31:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I think this needs to be fixed before PR1 - I noted this in the Status\nWhiteboard",
            "creator": "sol@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-12T17:12:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Scott,isThisFixedWithURLDispatching?",
            "creator": "phil@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-17T18:10:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Mail Review recommends not in this release.  Marking M20.",
            "creator": "selmer@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-05-06T17:43:21Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Moving target milestone to \"future\" to be reviewed at a later time",
            "creator": "lchiang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-08T05:48:14Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This works for imap:// at least. Maybe it's time to skim through the futured\nbugs and see what's still relevant.",
            "creator": "asa@mozilla.com",
            "timestamp": "2002-07-25T21:19:49Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "obsolete?",
            "creator": "vseerror@fastmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2005-11-28T18:33:27Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "(In reply to comment #17)\n> This works for imap:// at least. Maybe it's time to skim through the futured\n> bugs and see what's still relevant.\n\nimap://me@blah/Drafts doesn't do anything for me, nor does it produce an error message\n\nditto mailbox://\n\n",
            "creator": "vseerror@fastmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2006-07-07T21:51:52Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "As far as I can tell, this is a Seamonkey-type request. Feel free to move back if it isn't.",
            "creator": "Pidgeot18@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2008-06-19T19:24:01Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "WFM on Mozilla/5.0 (X11; U; Linux i686; de; rv:1.9pre) Gecko/2008050913 SeaMonkey/2.0a1pre",
            "creator": "kairo@kairo.at",
            "timestamp": "2008-07-03T23:58:48Z"
        }
    ]
}

{
    "bug_id": 5577,
    "summary": "GetFolder: Plugins target folder crashes",
    "comments": [
        {
            "text": "Build: 4/9/99 Engineering build\n\n1. Go to http://jimbob/jars and click on an8bitscript_plugins.jar\n\nRESULT:\nCrashes\n\nEXPECTED RESULT:\nBuilds folder \"charset\" as defined from GetFolder and populates with files as\ndescribed from install script.  There are target folders that work just fine.",
            "creator": "jimmykenlee@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-27T21:52:23Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Change QA Contact from Grace to Jimmy.",
            "creator": "jimmykenlee@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-27T21:54:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I have fixed the plugin target of GetFolder().  However, the jar still crashes\ndue to bug 6661.  I have modified the jar to work around this problem which\nverifies that this bug is fixed.  You can find this modified jar here:\n\nhttp://grok/u/dougt/xpinstall/an8bitscript_plugins_mod.jar",
            "creator": "doug.turner@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-18T17:36:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Build 6/1/99 Windows\n\n\n\nI'm crashing immediately when triggering the modified or the unmodified jar.  The\n\nInstall.log shows no entry triggered.  I am able to successfully trigger other\n\njar files.",
            "creator": "jimmykenlee@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-01T21:06:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Clearing Fixed resolution.",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-15T18:27:59Z"
        },
        {
