{
    "bug_id": 5579,
    "summary": "GetFolder: Crash when \"Current User\" is target folder",
    "comments": [
        {
            "text": "Build: 4/9/99 Engineering build\n\n1. Go to http://jimbob/jars and click on an8bitscript_currentuser.jar\n\nf = Install.GetFolder(\"Current User\", \"charset\");\n\nRESULT:\nCrash.  Log file shows no jar activity.\n\nEXPECTED RESULT:\nA folder named \"charset\" is created and populated with files as described from\ninstall script.  There are some target folders that work fine.",
            "creator": "jimmykenlee@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-27T22:04:58Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Changing QA Contact from Grace to Jimmy.",
            "creator": "jimmykenlee@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-04-27T22:05:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I have fixed the Current Usertarget of GetFolder().  However, the jar still\ncrashes due to bug 6661.  I have modified the jar to work around this problem\nwhich verifies that this bug is fixed.  You can find this modified jar here:\n\nhttp://grok/u/dougt/xpinstall/an8bitscript_currentuser_mod.jar",
            "creator": "doug.turner@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-18T17:42:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Build 6/1/99 Windows\n\nI am reopening this report to inquire why the modified jar fails while the\nunmodified one is now successful.\n\nI'm crashing immediately when triggering the modified jar.  However, unlike bug\nhttp://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=5577, triggering the unmodified jar\nnow behaves as expected.  The Install.log shows that the jar was triggered\nsuccesfully.",
            "creator": "jimmykenlee@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-01T21:21:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Clearing Fixed resolution.",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-15T18:27:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "setting target milestone to M10\n\nJimmy please verify if this is still an valid bug",
            "creator": "cathleennscp@netscape.net",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-16T01:33:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Build 6/16/99 SeaMonkey\n1. Go to http://puma/xpinstall/test/trigger.html (Samir's trigger--mine doesn't\n   work) and trigger\n   http://grok/u/dougt/xpinstall/an8bitscript_currentuser_mod.jar\n\nI still get a crash on Windows NT. However, a \"charset\" folder is created as\nexpected and files are added up to \"ch170-?\".\n\nTalkback Incident ID = TB10115022Q\n\nCall Stack: (Signature = 0x0012fe80 fc18e85d)\n\n0x0012fe80\n0x00720072\n0x02000003",
            "creator": "jimmykenlee@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-17T18:42:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Build 7/30/99\n\nThis still crashes for me with an8bitscript_currentuser_mod.jar.  The folder is\ncreated and some files are added.  This time it got as far as ch204-Þ.  Linux\nand Macintosh behave fine.  The problem is only with WindowsNT and Win98.  Win95\nhas not been tested yet.\n\nTalkback Incident ID = 11924298\nCall Stack: (Signature = 0x0012fe6c 17a3bc81)\n0x0012fe6c\n\nhttp://cyclone/reports/incidenttemplate.CFM?reportID=124&style=0&tc=8&cp=1&ck1=S\nUser+email+address&cd1=%25jimmylee%40netscape%2Ecom%25&co1=like&bbid=11924298",
            "creator": "jimmykenlee@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-02T21:00:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Clearing WorksForMe resolution due to reopen.",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-03T00:27:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "okay.  I can not reproduce this again.  Lets do the basic problem thing and\nbreak this bug into pieces.\n\nFirst, lets remove all the 8 bit characters from this jar file.  Let make a\nsimple case that does an add of 1000 files into current user.\n\nSecond, lets make a test case that only adds 8bit files.\n\nThird, lets get together so that you can show me the crash.  I maybe able to\nfigure out more than the talkback report.\n\nUntil then, I will leave as assigned.",
            "creator": "doug.turner@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-13T01:20:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "After talking to cathleen, she comfirms that the problem really is the 8bit\ncharacters.  This 8 bit problem, only occurs on optimized builds.  I am closing\n*this* bug as WORKS_FOR_ME and advise opening a new bug regarding the 8bit\ncharacters.",
            "creator": "doug.turner@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-16T05:44:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Marking this verified.  Will investigate 8-bit characters further and enter new\nbug if necessary.",
            "creator": "jimmykenlee@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-25T01:14:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Bulk move of XPInstall (component to be deleted) bugs to Installer: XPInstall\nEngine",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-08T23:39:59Z"
        }
    ]
}

{
    "bug_id": 6053,
    "summary": "[PP][key][DOGFOOD] Windows 95, 98 Dead keys nonfunctional",
    "comments": [
        {
            "text": "This applies to any text input widget in M5. I cannot test this on any other platform. If you run Win98 with the keyboard layout set to US-International (with so-called dead keys) entering a dead key results in a space being displayed. You can still past text that contains quotes, just not enter them in apprunner directly. This is not covered in the Release notes or Bugzilla as far as I can see...",
            "creator": "gelderen@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T21:48:36Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This is invalid. I use that keyboard layout on a daily basis and have had no\nproblems.",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T22:08:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Marking verified. The following line is typed using the US-International keyboard layout:\n\nIch weiß nicht was soll es bedeuten / Daß ich so traurig bin\nEin Märchen aus alten Zeiten...",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T22:11:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I am reopening this bug because the fact that you don t see the problem doesnt not imply that it does not exist. I am available if you need more information, Im just not sure what more I could give you.",
            "creator": "gelderen@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T22:12:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "gelderen, I'm not sure what else to check. I have tested against Windows 98 on\na H-P Vectra and Compaq Deskpro 590, and against Windows NT 4 on a Vectra and\nDell Dimension XPS R400.\n\nWhen you say it 'doesn't work', in what sense doesn't it work? Can you be more\nspecific? On all of these machines, I am able to click into any field and type\nanything with the US-International keyboard layout. And you are familiar with\nthe quirks of this layout, right? (ie you have to press '-space to get the '\ncharacter, and so on...)",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T22:21:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Clearing target milestone (M5 is a past milestone and therefore not valid).",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T22:22:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "btw, what do you mean by 'dead key'? (i just noticed that part of your original\ncomment, sorry - and yes, we're working on better textarea wrapping in mozilla!)",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T22:27:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "When Im typing, the dead keys are simply ignored. (Normally a single quote would have showed up in the second word of the sentence.) If I type a dead key followed by a space (to force display) all I get is a space. Copying that space into a hex-editor reveals that it really is a space (0x20).\n\nAt the same time, dead keys work in my other programs, just not in Apprunner. The machine is an IBM Thinkpad 560Z Running Win98 (4.10.1998). I can give a dump of the Microsoft System Information if thats helpful. I can also run diagnostics if you have something that could be helpful.\n\nNow a weird thing that I just noticed is that sometimes entering a single quote indeed shows a single quote. I cannot reliably reproduce this, however :-(\n\nIll try and find out more of course...",
            "creator": "gelderen@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T22:32:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Oops, accidentally reverted the milestone. Back to M15 it is...",
            "creator": "gelderen@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T22:33:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "A dead key is a key that does nothing when typed (single quote, double quote,\napostroph, tilde). It will only show up when the next character is entered.\nThis is from typewriter idiom where a dead key would appear but not\nadvance(wording?) paper.\n\nJust to check: arrow keys and 'end  and delete dont work either on my machine.\nAnd as you can see in the previous sentence slipped trough. Weird...\n\nChatting in Bugzilla is fun, isnt it? ;-)",
            "creator": "gelderen@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T22:38:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Lo and behold, I was wrong. There are indeed problems with the US-International keyboard layout on Windows 98. (My problem was that my keying method was different that gelderen's.)\n\nHere's how to reproduce this:\nbuild ID: 1999050408\nplatform: windows 98\n\n1. Set your keyboard layout to US-International in the Keyboard control panel.\n2. Open a page in apprunner with a text input control. Start typing. Try these things:\nUsing the US-International keyboard layout requires you to press Space after typing certain characters to type the character itself. For example, if you type the single quote key, if you type a after it, you get á. If you type a space after it, you should get '. However, behavior is very inconsistent in apprunner. To get that single quote, I had to type single quote-space about ten times and then delete the extra spaces.\nAdditionally, when you follow a modifier (what gelderen calls a dead key, I think) with a letter that cannot be modified by it - for example, if you follow the single quote with the letter M - you should then get 'm. So...\n\nIn apprunner:\n\nFollowing a modifier or dead key with a space should give you the modifier itself. Try this with the tilde, single quote, or double quote (to name a few). However, it usually just gives you a space.\n\nFollowing a modifier with a character it can't modify should insert the modifier and the character into the text input control - for example, typing I-single quote-m should give you I'm. However, apprunner usually gives you Im.\n\nI have been unable to reproduce this on NT, but it's definitely a problem under Windows 98.\n\ngelderen, thanks for the bug. in the future, however, please be as explicit as possible... prevents us from closing things just based on the summary line! :)",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T22:47:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "FYI: I was running build 1999050423 when I detected the problem.",
            "creator": "gelderen@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T23:08:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Tague, I'm going to send this your way.  I don't know if it belongs to you and\nwidget code you're doing or to some widget guy like Rod.  I'll let you reassign\nas necessary.",
            "creator": "joki@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T23:19:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "okie - i'll try to take a look at this soon.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-06T23:21:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "this should be fixed with the patch that i am waiting on.  the patch fixes a\nbunch of things related to keyboard input, i18n, unicode, and windows.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-22T03:44:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "bulk move to M9",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-08T20:36:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Was this fixed by Tague's key event fixes from last Sunday?",
            "creator": "bobj@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-16T10:00:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "No, it was not fixed. I just tested build 1999061509-win32 with a bugzilla form.",
            "creator": "gelderen@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-16T10:14:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Can you please verify this against Ender.  Right now, we/I am not supporting the\nnon-Ender text widgets since they are going to go away.  I would like to know if\n(and i'm assuming it has been) the problem has been fixed for Ender - or if I\nhave more work to do there.\n\nIf it's fixed for Ender, we can close it out once Ender replaces the other text\nwidgets.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-16T18:23:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "moving to M10 to reflect bug fixing priorities",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-09T21:38:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "could someone please verify if this problem occurs with ender or not",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-20T21:02:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Using the 1999071908 build under Windows 98, this problem happens in Ender.",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-20T21:18:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I'm not sure when this changed, but this is now no longer working on Windows NT\nas well (I used SP 5). Upgrading severity as this negatively impacts my ability\nto use the product.",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-27T21:58:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "can you give me a detailed script of what you are trying and the unexpected\nresults that you are getting.\n\nthis is getting tested with russian, greek, german, and japanese keyboard and i\nhaven't seen any incorrect behavior.\n\ni will look at this over the weekend to see if someone hasn't introduced a\nregression.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-28T01:35:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "tague, the bug described in this bugzilla report has never been\nfixed AFAIK. Look at the 11th description 'lo and behold...' to\nsee exactly how you can reproduce this problem.\n\nYou don't need weird keyboards for this, just use a US keyboard\nand set it's layout to US-International (in the Control Panel\non Windoze). Then try and type some text with accents in any\ntext field (for example in the URL-textfield).\n\nI've verified that this problem still exists in Apprunner\n1999082412 on Win98 (4.10.1998) and I have never seen a\nbuild in which it was fixed. I check builds daily or every\nother day.",
            "creator": "gelderen@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-28T11:25:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "tague, this has never worked for me on Windows 9x, and as of late it isn't\nworking for me on Windows NT either.\n\nOnce again, to reproduce:\n\n1. In Windows, select Settings | Control Menu from the Start menu.\n2. Open the Keyboard control panel.\n3. Click the Language tab.\n4. Click the Properties button.\n5. Change the Keyboard Layout to US-International.\n6. Click OK. Click OK again to exit the Keyboard control panel.\n7. Launch Notepad and the latest build of Apprunner.\n8. In Notepad, try typing words with accents/diacritics, eg naïve, San José,\nschön, sueño. You do this by typing a dead key followed by the character you\nwish to modify - eg ~+n = ñ, \"+o = ö, '+e = é.\n9. Switch to Apprunner. Select File | New | Blank Window to bring up an Editor\nwindow. Now, try typing those same words.\n\nResult: you can't. You just get blank spaces instead of the desired characters.\nIf that isn't specific enough, please call me (check the internal company\nphonebook) and I would be happy to do a demo for you next week. Thanks!",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-28T18:31:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "thanks that helps alot - i kept trying to fix something different. sorry for all\nthe trouble.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-28T18:41:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 12393 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-31T02:19:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "okay, it should be fixed now.  i got confused about the us-international layout\nthing, i thought you were refering to different keyboard layouts in general\n(like on the mac) not dead keys.\n\ni tested this with various combinations of acute, grave, and tildes.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-31T09:10:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Today's build (1999083108) crashes whenever I click into the body of the\nbrowser; until that bug (12743) is fixed I can't verify this fix.",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-31T22:07:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I will verify as soon as the Windoze builds work again.\n(They crash on startup now.)",
            "creator": "gelderen@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-01T15:14:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Reopening and clearing resolution.\n\nUsing the 1999090208 build under Windows 98, I saw these results:\n\n- Typing \"-o displayed 'o' and not 'ö'\n- Typing RightAlt-S displayed nothing and not 'ß'\n\nIn short, it hasn't been fixed.",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-02T17:46:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "As off today I can no longer type dots (.) in any text control in Mozilla.\nThis is 1999091914 on Win98. I'm not filing this in a new bug because it\nmay be related. It certainly is annoying :-)",
            "creator": "gelderen@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-20T22:37:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Underscores (_) are also AWOL in current builds, but those are covered in\nanother bug. Periods are working under NT but I'm not sure about 95/98...\ngelderen, I'd just as soon file another bug on the periods if that's\nreproducible on the 1999092013 build under 95/98 (if tague et alii have nothing\nagainst it). Thanks again, gelderen, for your continued contributions!",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-20T22:40:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I spoke too soon it appears, dots work in 1999092008. That version however\nrefuses to show me http://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=6053 :-) Oh well,\ntyping in 4.6 now, I'll keep an eye on in and maybe file a bug later.\n\nI'm now going to look for a bug describing the alt-tab problem as that drives me\ncrazy during browsing. (The problem being that on alt-tab to another Mozilla\nwindow, the File menu is selected. This wouldn't be a problem if it weren't for\nthe fact that clicking in the Location field doesn't switch the input focus ->\nevery character you type now selects a menu. Grr.) I think the latter problem\nmay be related to this bug too...",
            "creator": "gelderen@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-20T22:57:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "tague is doomed, reassign this to myself.",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-01T20:39:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Mark it as M11 assigned. Add tague/buster/akkana/rods to the cc list",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-01T22:37:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I look at this bug and try it with the fix I have for bug 15657/11845. The part\nwhich is still broken is AltGR and Shift+AltGR.",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-08T22:57:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "fix in keyEvent_19991004_BRANCH landing",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-14T20:12:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Using the 1999101411 build under Windows NT, both dead keys and the AltGR key\nwork (nearly) as expected (well, the AltGR key also activates the menus, but\nthis is great!).\n\nUsing the same build under Windows 98, dead keys do NOT work in text input\ncontrols, but the AltGR key does (but the menus are dropped down as well, alas).\n\nSo, this is NOT fixed in the 1999101411 build under Windows 98. ftang, in which\nbuild should this be fixed?",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-14T22:01:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 10901 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-14T22:04:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Using the 1999101508 build under Windows 98, dead keys are still broken\n(although the AltGR key is working OK). Reopening and clearing resolution.",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-15T17:35:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "cpratt:\n1.Can the follow table represent the current status ?\nWinNT: AltGr Work. Dead Key Work\nWin98:AltGr Work. Dead Key Does Not Work\nWin95: ?\n\n2. Can you try this keyboard with NotePad in Win98 and see does the dead key work there ?",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-15T18:01:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "In response:\n\n> 1.Can the follow table represent the current status ?\n> WinNT: AltGr Work. Dead Key Work\n> Win98:AltGr Work. Dead Key Does Not Work\n> Win95: ?\n\nYes, that's it. AltGR now works on all Win32 OSes. Dead keys are not working on\nWindows 98 or Windows 95.\n\n> 2. Can you try this keyboard with NotePad in Win98 and\n> see does the dead key work there?\n\nThe dead keys work as expected in every other Windows app I've tried, including\nNotepad.",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-15T18:05:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "down grade the problem to P2 M11",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-15T23:02:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "M12? This is a VERY important problem for non-US English users...",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-28T16:18:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "ok... change it to M11...",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-28T16:39:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Putting on [PDT+] radar.",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-29T00:37:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "In message composition in M11- 29/10/99  release under Windows 98, when\ncompoosing a message in Greek (ISO-8859-7), the keyboard key for accents is not\nfunctional.In text composition the accent  ´  (which is the accent in greek and\nsupposed to appear after entering a character) do not works at all.",
            "creator": "kpapadim@arafin.gr",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-31T12:02:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "really do not have time to fix for M11. Move to M12",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-03T22:57:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "You might want to ask the Javasoft folks as I seem to recall having\nseen the same bug in their early AWT implementations. Sorry for not\nhaving more details :-((\n\n(But happily writing CSS2 pages that actually *work* :-))",
            "creator": "idefix@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-10T19:56:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I don't know why the behavior is different between NT and 9x, but are we\nhandling the sequence of messages correctly:\n  WM_KEYDOWN\n  WM_DEADCHAR\n  WM_KEYUP\n  WM_KEYDOWN\n  WM_CHAR\n  WM_KEYUP\n\nAccording to http://msdn.microsoft.com/library/psdk/winui/keybinpt_7r1v.htm,\ntypically an app ignores the 1st 2 messages and then process the 2nd wm_char\nmessage.  I searched http://lxr.mozilla.org/seamonkey/ and did not find any\nwm_deadchar.  Do we need to catch these messages and clear up state from the\nwm_keydown?",
            "creator": "bobj@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-10T21:15:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Un(fortunately) I don't have a Windoze SDK installed, but I\nexperimented a little in the URL box in the latest Mozilla build.\n\nIt appears that the dead keys are 'eaten' by Mozilla, unless you\ntype fast. If you rapidly enter a sequence of 'e' followed by\n'\\'' you'll see something like \"eeeeeeeeeééeeeeeeeéééééééeeee\"\nappearing.\n\nCan fast typing lead to losing WM_XXXX messages? Will a lost\nmessage lead to the showing up of 'é's?\n\nMaybe-related-note:\nSDK says that WM_CHAR is generated after WM_KEYDOWN but\nWM_DEADCHAR is generated upon WM_KEYUP.\n\nhttp://leb.net/wine/WinDoc/msdn/sdk/platforms/doc/sdk/win32/mess/src/msg23_13.ht\nm\nhttp://leb.net/wine/WinDoc/msdn/sdk/platforms/doc/sdk/win32/mess/src/msg22_9.htm",
            "creator": "idefix@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-10T21:54:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Is this a problem with how we are processing the WM_xxx event messages on\nWindows in\n   http://lxr.mozilla.org/seamonkey/source/widget/src/windows/nsWindow.cpp\n\nReassigning to joki.",
            "creator": "bobj@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-11T21:01:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Sorry it took me a couple days to get to this again but I don't think this is\nmine. I handle events passed in from the widget library.  If the bug is in the\nwidget library it belongs to widget people which would be either the Windows\nwidget library owner, a rather fuzzy category right now but it was rods for a\nwhile, or i18n people working on the Windows widget library which I believe is\nftang.  For now I'll go back to ftang as he worked on this stuff last.",
            "creator": "joki@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-16T19:55:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I know I should own this bug. I don't know why bobj reassign this to joki.",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-16T21:40:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I am not sure this is different from NT and 95/98 now. I can see the same\nproblem in my NT on Location bar now. It is fine on my NT in form, composer,\nplatin text editor. cpratt- could you try this again in your 95/98 against\n1. URL bar\n2. text field in html form\n3. composer\n4. plain text editor\n5. Mail subject field ?",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-16T23:51:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "cpratt out for two weeks, eli can you help frank out?",
            "creator": "paulmac@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-17T00:01:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Build: 1999111408 on Win98\n1. URL bar - buggy\n2. text field in html form - buggy (bugzilla, enter email address)\n3. composer - crash, can't test\n4. plain text editor - crash, can't test\n5. Mail subject field ? - crash, can't test",
            "creator": "gelderen@mediaport.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-17T00:05:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "[Sure. QA Assigning to self, due to cpratt's vacation + change of role.]",
            "creator": "elig@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-17T00:12:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Frank, does the information provided by gelderen@mediaport.org answer your cpratt\nquery, or would you like me to check using a newer build? Thanks!",
            "creator": "elig@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-23T18:17:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "It should fix now. Please test again.",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-01T22:13:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Verified fixed in Composer using the 1999120315 build under Windows 98.\ngelderen, if you see this anywhere else... thanks!",
            "creator": "cpratt@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-06T18:55:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Commit pushed to master at https://github.com/mozilla/bedrock\n\nhttps://github.com/mozilla/bedrock/commit/bebad9158aa8795a45f2a4025a0f1f8d953192e4\nAdd channel to system requirements page title\n\nmozilla/bedrock/issues#6053",
            "creator": "mozilla+bugcloser@davedash.com",
            "timestamp": "2018-08-27T15:30:01Z"
        }
    ]
}

{
    "bug_id": 6625,
    "summary": "Redundant rules in ua.css",
    "comments": [
        {
            "text": "The following rules are redundant in ua.css:

 Lines 192, 198, 216, 221: The "a" in the selector is redundant and should be
 removed. This is especially important for future proofing (eventually,
 elements other than "a" will be able to be links).

 Lines 204 and 226: The "a" in the selector should be changed for ":link" and
 ":visited", so that the selectors read
    :link:active, :visited:active
 ...instead of
    a:active
 This is also for future proofing (see first comment).

 Lines 192-203: the "display", "text-decoration" and "cursor" declarations of
 the :link and :visited rules could be merged, like this:
    :link, :visited {
      display: inline;
      text-decoration: underline;
      cursor: pointer;
    }

 Lines 204-209: the "display", "text-decoration" and "cursor" declarations
 can be completely removed as they are specified by the :link and :visited
 rules, because the :active pseudo-class is concurrent with the :link and
 :visited pseudo-classes.

 Lines 219, 224, 229, 234: the "text-decoration:none" declarations will have
 no effect as the underlining from the surrounding links would "override"
 it anyway.

 Lines 216-235: All these rules can actually be collapsed into a single rule:
    :link img, :visited img {
       border: 2px solid;
    }
 This is because the :active pseudo-class is concurrent with the :link and
 :visited pseudo-classes, and the other declarations can be removed as
 described in the comments above.

 Many lines: The "display:inline" declarations on many of the lines are
 completely superfluous as that is the default value of the display property.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-18T01:16:30Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Some other changes that could be made to increase the efficiency and decrease
the size of the ua.css:

1. line-height:normal does not need to be defined in the body; it is the default

2. Instead of defining every hx element separately, like
h1 {
  display: block;
  font-size: xx-large;
  font-weight: bold;
  margin: 1em 0;
}

h2 {
  display: block;
  font-size: x-large;
  font-weight: bold;
  margin: 1em 0;
}
it would be better to use
h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 {
  display: block;
  margin: 1em 0;
  font-weight: bold;
}
and then define the font size for each header separately.

3. Elements without any styles applied to them don't need to be included at
all.  This would include abbr, acronym, del, dfn, ins, q, span; spacer, wbr, :-
moz-text, img; :-moz-line-frame, and :-moz-letter-frame.

4. Instead of defining each to be displayed as a block-level element
seperately, group :cell-content, :fieldset-content, :button-content, :label-
content together with {display: block}. Do the same with {display: none}
elements.

5. Use shorthand. Instead of
  {padding-left  : 2px;
   padding-right : 2px;
   padding-top   : 1px;
   padding-bottom: 1px;}
use
  {padding: 1px 2px;}

6. For input[type=hidden], dont use {border-none}. The {visibility: none} takes
care of the border.

(Another thing: a 1px border on iframe looks better than a 2px border. The
entire interface of apprunner looks much cleaner.)",
            "creator": "allen.sam@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-30T02:13:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Created attachment 302
Cleaned-up ua.css (3k smaller than the current one!)",
            "creator": "allen.sam@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-30T02:16:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Another thing:

There are two iframe rules.  They should be combined to read

iframe {
  background-color: white;
  border: 2px solid black;
}

or even better, border: 1px solid black.",
            "creator": "allen.sam@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-05-30T02:22:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Deferring this. These are all good suggestions, but as time goes by the file
will get messy again. I'll make a cleanup pass shortly before ship.",
            "creator": "peterl-retired@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-10T22:52:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Regarding comment no 2 from Additional Comments From bloviate@yahoo.com
05/29/99 19:13:

In CSS1 specification it is noted that the em is a relative unit and will be
inherited as the computed value therefor also the margin setting should be in
the seperate H1, H2 etc. rules",
            "creator": "hans.aikema@aikebah.net",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-11T02:56:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Reassigning peterl's bugs to myself.",
            "creator": "pierre@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-21T08:07:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Accepting peterl's bugs that have a Target Milestone",
            "creator": "pierre@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-21T08:12:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Let's consider this a Time&Space problem... Reassigned to attinasi.
FYI, we have 270 rules in html.css and 78 in xul.css.
",
            "creator": "pierre@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-03-02T07:03:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I believe that Peter's comment that this need to be cleaned-up before shipping 
is valid, however I also think we should make any valid changes incrementally, 
as we come across them, so we are getting the most possible testing time with 
the most relevent rules in place (reduced risk). Thus, I'll start applying these 
changes soon after beta.

NOTE: reducing the number and size of rules helps lower the memory impact of the 
StyleSytem and can thus be considered an optimization, which is a benefit in 
addition to that of increasing the maintainability of the document by reducing 
redundancy.",
            "creator": "attinasi@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-03-03T17:22:06Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Marc: Would you prefer if I went through the html.css file and filed bugs with
diffs one change at a time, or would you rather I went through html.css and
quirks.css and neatened them both up in one go, and then attached the new files
to this bug (or the quirks.css bug) directly?
",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-05T23:56:28Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "If you find quirk-rules that should be fixed for PR2 then I suggest you pull 
them out into separate (new) bugs. General cleanup can go into one big change 
for this bug. My reasoning is that the PDT team is more likely to approve 
changes that are small and discrete, and some of the changes are not related to 
quirk vs. standard mode rule migrations (i.e. they are general cleanup). Sound 
OK Ian? Thanks!",
            "creator": "attinasi@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-06T01:10:34Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "OK - I guess we shouldn't put this off any longer, it is time to clean the mess 
up and watch the changes that get checked in from now through RTM",
            "creator": "attinasi@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-24T23:22:13Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "The easiest way to do this would be to lock that file, then I could spend a day
cleaning it up and browsing to make sure nothing broke, and then we could get 
the file checked in. But this requires the file to have absolutely no changes
made to it, otherwise they will be lost.

Alternatively, we can do this one patch at a time, but that would take ages...",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-24T23:26:08Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "For performance, the key is to reduce the number of selectors.",
            "creator": "dbaron@dbaron.org",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-24T23:28:16Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Do we want to optimize the rules (selectors) as well as remove the redundant 
ones? That seems like a higher risk proposal, and maybe that should be a 
seperate task done after the redundancy is eliminated.

Ian, I'll look into locking a file: presumable html.css and quirk.css, or do 
you want to attack xu.css too? Even if we cannot lock them, I think the rate of 
change is very low now (last change to html.css was mid-June) so we should be 
able to manage the update with little more than a heads-up email and posting to 
the community.",
            "creator": "attinasi@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-24T23:40:11Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I don't know enough about the magic behind the xul selectors to edit xul.css
as well, so best leave that one alone.

Similarly for parts of html.css like this:

   :-moz-line-frame {

   }

   :-moz-letter-frame {
   }

I assume I either just leave those alone or put them in a comment block at 
the end of the file?
",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-24T23:45:28Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Hmmm. What good is an empty rule? Maybe it is a reminder? In that case a 
comment-block is probably best. 

Also, since Hyatt has considerable expertiese, and since he is best equipped to 
handle the xul.css file, I'm CC'ing him on this (I believe he has already tuned 
xul.css anyway).",
            "creator": "attinasi@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-24T23:52:57Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Approving for nsbeta3: this needs to be doen prior to beta3 completion and then 
locked down for FCS.",
            "creator": "attinasi@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-31T18:34:20Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Marc - I suggest we postpone this until just after nsbeta2 ships. Until then I
will be very busy blessing builds and so on.

Once nsbeta2 ships, we'll need to announce the ua.css|html.css|quirks.css
lockdown and once that is done I can start going through the stylesheets.

Also, when you have a moment could you list the best ways of optimising the
stylesheets? David mentioned using grouping as much as possible, for example.
",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-31T20:03:19Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Ian, I agree that this should wait a while - in fact, we could wait until the 
very end of the beta3 cycle (we should leave a week for testing though).

I really don't think we should be optimizing too much - the focus should be on 
removing redundancy and eliminating rules that have no affect. That will be a 
performance gain too. The more we restructure rules the more risk we take on, so 
we should minimize that activity and focus on size reduction by elimination of 
cruft and duplication.",
            "creator": "attinasi@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-31T20:37:16Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Even though the risk level is low, it is wide ranging since this affects _all_
HTML content. So I'd rather we did it nearer the start of the beta3 cycle.
Also, if we leave it to the end then I'll be doing beta3 blessing so we'll have
run out of time again. ;-)

Question: Is it a bad thing to have more stylesheets linked in from ua.css? I
was considering moving the viewsource styles out into another file (they are a
bit out of place in html.css). Would that be a bad thing?",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-31T20:43:19Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Each imported stylesheet has to be file-opened, but other than that I don't 
think that there is a problem with importing viewsource.css into us.css (for 
example).

As for the timing comments - makes perfect sense :)",
            "creator": "attinasi@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-31T21:18:24Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I'm on it.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-08-22T02:15:04Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I'm also trying to move lots of the quirky rules in html.css to quirk.css. We'll
see how that goes.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-08-22T02:16:01Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "PDT would like to know: is this going to make a real impact on performance or
footprint? Or are we just beautifying? Cleanup can wait until after seamonkey.",
            "creator": "phil@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-08-30T23:30:19Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "It will have some impact; how much I do not know, it would need measuring.
The work is almost all done anyway, I just need to check it through the top100
and the various CSS test suites before passing it back to Marc for checkin.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-08-30T23:41:17Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "The potential for speed and size improvements are good. Rules take up a lot of 
memory, and processing rules takes a lot of time in page layout and event 
handling, so by lowering the number of rules we should be able to get both a 
speed-up and a memory savings, proportional to the percentage decrease in the 
number of rules - roughly speaking.",
            "creator": "attinasi@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-08-30T23:47:36Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "There are some very inefficient rules in html.css that would have an impact on 
style resolution performance of form elements... e.g., stuff like

td input {

}

slows down all input controls.  This is bad.
",
            "creator": "hyatt@mozilla.org",
            "timestamp": "2000-08-31T00:16:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Part of my fix has been to move these rules into quirk.css. Thus only pages
using compat mode will be affected. I may remove _some_ of those rules 
altogether, depending on whether I can find why they were put in in the first
place.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-08-31T00:22:01Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Ok, I'm about to mail out the proposed new files to everyone who has claimed
they want to review the changes before this is checked in. ;-)",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-09-14T13:30:57Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Ian, ua.css is at risk of not making the commercial product.  We really need the 
changes to be stable and landed very, very soon.  Once we start stabilizing for 
RTM, only super serious P1's will make the cut.",
            "creator": "clayton@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-09-19T23:58:26Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Ian, wasn't this checked in?  Please reopen if I'm mistaken, thanks.",
            "creator": "bugzilla@blakeross.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-09-22T03:06:45Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "blake: Yes, it was checked in. However, I had to check that what was checked in
was what I had written before marking it fixed. Since you did not know what my
final files looked like, you could not check that, so should not have marked
it fixed.

Having said that: FIXED by halving the size of html.css. Bloat is down a not
inconsiderable amount according to Marc's figures.

ChrisD: To verify, examine the ua.css, quirk.css and html.css files that are
present in the distribution and check that they are now considerably smaller 
than in previous days.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-09-22T05:16:55Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Per Ian's suggestion, verified that html.css is approximately cut in half. 
Marking bug verified",
            "creator": "christinehoff4@aol.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-09-27T20:02:28Z"
        }
    ]
}

{
    "bug_id": 7640,
    "summary": "BMW: mailto: url and encoding (can't switch unix mail encoding)",
    "comments": [
        {
            "text": "(This bug imported from BugSplat, Netscape's internal bugsystem.  It\nwas known there as bug #118092\nhttp://scopus.netscape.com/bugsplat/show_bug.cgi?id=118092\nImported into Bugzilla on 06/04/99 18:07)\n\nBroken out from a 3 part ESCALATION.  This one is just for the Unix encoding\nswitch bug.\nSee http://scopus.mcom.com/bugsplat/show_bug.cgi?id=117748\n  When using the DS gateway, if client accept UTF-8 character, charset is\n  automatically set to UTF-8. After a search, when you click on the mailto: url,\n\n  the charset is to UTF-8 automatically in mail composition.\n  On NT communicator crashes when assembling the mail.\n  On WIN95 the mail is sent and arrives in UTF-7 encoding\n  On unix you cannot change the encoding\n\n  Customer (BMW) doesn't accept the workaround to change charset when the mail\n  is received.\n  Customer would like mail creation to be set to ISO-8859-1 after the mailto:\n  url regardless of the charset of the page it is coming from.\n  Or it could be automatically detected and correctly displayed as asked in bug\n  #113344?",
            "creator": "bobj@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1998-05-15T14:00:00Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I looked at this with the AIX 4.1 5/15/98 build.\nThe behavior there is that the DS Gateway encoding (UTF-8) has no effect on\nwhat the new mail composer window is:\n\nIf the user has set the encoding menu to \"Western\" while viewing the\nNS Phonebook page, for example, the new composer menu shows \"Western\".\n\nIf the user has set the encoding menu to \"Japanese\" while viewing the\nNS Phonebook page, for example, the new composer menu shows \"Japanese\".\n\nThis seems to be different from the Windows behavior where the Gateway's\nencoding has an effect on the new composer window's encoding choice.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1998-05-19T20:30:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "By the way, Mac 4.05-RTM has the same behavior as the Unix 4.1.\nSo Windows is the only one behaving the way this bug describes.\nBefore going further, can we have a consistent story across the platforms?",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1998-05-19T20:34:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Assigning it to erik.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1998-05-19T20:35:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Could afalight clarify what is meant by \"On unix you cannot change the\nencoding\". This does not seem to be the behavior I've found so far.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1998-05-19T20:43:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "When you are reading a mail, there is no menu available to change the encoding\nlike on PC and MAC (under view/encoding).",
            "creator": "afalight@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1998-05-20T12:51:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Thanks. It is correct that we are lacking an encoding menu for\nplain text mail on Unix. There was a bug filed for this earlier.\nAdding dora to distribution. Hopefully she remembers what happened to\nthe Plain Mial Window's encoding menu.\n\nThis is not a problem for HTML mail since there is a functioning\nencoding menu in that window.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1998-05-21T01:41:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "XFE is missing some of its UI. We need to assign this to an XFE engineer.",
            "creator": "erik@vanderpoel.org",
            "timestamp": "1998-06-11T00:19:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Changing TFV to 5.0.",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1998-06-11T01:27:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I'm getting of all bugs to be fixed in 5.0 off the Nova radar. We'll revisit\nthem later. --- Marek",
            "creator": "bugz@jeziorek.com",
            "timestamp": "1998-08-05T19:29:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Re-opening bug after Marek resovled it as remind.",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1998-08-25T07:32:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "adding myself to cc: list.",
            "creator": "lchiang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1998-09-01T22:19:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "PSE assigned",
            "creator": "raym@obsolete.mozilla.org",
            "timestamp": "1998-11-10T00:01:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Kat, I'd like to move this over to bugzilla.  Can you summarize for me what the\nproblem is that we'd have to fix/check for in 5.0?  I don't want to move this\ndirectly to bugzilla since a customer's name (BMW) is mentioned.  Thanks.\n\n(Also, this bug shouldn't be mcafee's since it's in mail.)",
            "creator": "lchiang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-02-10T23:06:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "The problem seems to be two-fold.\n\n1. We are not intializing the text body charset correctly when the\n   underlying page is in UTF-8 and the mail composer is in plain text.\n\n   (HTML mail seems to be working OK.)\n\n2. There is no Character set menu for the plain text mail composer.\n\nThese problems arise out of the Communicator 4 code base. In Communicator 5,\nthese problems should not be there at all as we should be able to\nhandle HTML and plain text mail via the same code.\n\nSo we should either fix this bug in 4.5x, or close it out with a reminder\nthat we should check in 5.x that this does not happen. Let me dispose of\nthis bug when I do 4.xx review later this week.\n\n------- Additional Comments From alecf  May-25-1999 14:22 -------\n\nWhy is this assigned to me? Escalation => marek\n\n------- Additional Comments From marek  Jun-04-1999 16:02 -------\n\nWe are not going to fix it in 4.x. Ther ought to be a bug in bugzilla to deal\nwith this.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-02-11T03:49:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I think this is a reminder bug for 5.0 Messenger on Unix. For now, I'm re-assigning this to nhotta.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-05T04:25:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Accepting the bug. I think we only need a verification once mailto url is\navailable for 5.0 (adding rhp@netscape.com to cc).\nThe current plan for a charset of a new mail (including mailto) is to use the\none in the pref file (intl.character_set_name or map to mail charset in case it\nis not a mail charset e.g. Shift_JIS).",
            "creator": "nhottanscp@yahoo.co.jp",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-08T00:05:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "mailto is not yet available, marking as M8 for now.",
            "creator": "nhottanscp@yahoo.co.jp",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-08T21:19:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Adding dependency to 5247 and moved to M9.",
            "creator": "nhottanscp@yahoo.co.jp",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-21T18:33:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Move to M10, necko has not landed so far.",
            "creator": "nhottanscp@yahoo.co.jp",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-22T23:23:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "M11 since 5247 is M11.",
            "creator": "nhottanscp@yahoo.co.jp",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-12T21:25:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "M15",
            "creator": "nhottanscp@yahoo.co.jp",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-09T21:38:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "mail to url is enabled.\nA charset mailtourl is the same as the new mail's charset (pref's default\ncharset or ISO-8895-1 if no pref specified).\nNeed cross platform verificatin.",
            "creator": "nhottanscp@yahoo.co.jp",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-23T19:01:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "** Checked with 12/8/99 Win32 build **\n\nThe observed behvaior is as explained by nhotta with the above build.\n\n0. I used\n\n   user_pref(\"intl.character_set_name\",\"iso-2022-jp\");\n\n as the test prefs.js line.\n\n1. I used the above URL page (which is encoded in UTF-8)\n   and clicked on the mailto link and sent out mail with some\n   text in it.\n2. I also deleted the above line and tried step 1.\n\nIn case of 1, I got mail in \"iso-2022-jp\" -- header was mime-encoded\ncorrectly and the body was encoded in iso-2022-jp.\n\nIn case of 2, I received the message in iso-8859-1.\n\nSo these are expected results.\n\nThe fix is verified under Win32.\n(There was an unrelated problem to this fix with the mailtoutl\n but this will be filed as a separate bug.)\n\nji, please verify this using the same method for Linux.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-09T09:40:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Note that if there is a checkmark or some indication in the\nCharacter Set menu, we don't have to send out a message to\nfind out if this is working but currently the menu has no\nfeedback as to what charset it is using to send out mail.\n\nAlso, Netscape internal Phonebook would be a good test case\nbut the Phonebook search does not work well with Mozilla at\npresent.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-09T09:44:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Over to ji for Linux verification. When you're done,\nmark this verified/fixed.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-15T07:39:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Apparently I forgot to paste in the test URL.\nI just did so. Use this one at the URL field.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-15T18:26:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "With Linux 1999121508 build, in case 1, which doesn't have charset name in the\nprefs.js file, I sent out a mail from the above URL in plain text mode. The\nheader is MIME-encoded correctly, but the body is encoded in us-ascii. Please\nsee the attachment for the source of the mail.",
            "creator": "ji@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-15T18:55:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Created attachment 3512\nsource file for the mail sent w/o charset name in prefs.js",
            "creator": "ji@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-15T18:57:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "You didn't include 8-bit characters in the body, right?\nIn that case, US-ASCII is expected. If the body includes\nreal 8-bit characters, you shoudl get ISO-8859-1.\nThe header contains 8-bit characters and so bears\niso-8859-1.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-15T19:09:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Yes, you are right. After i inluded 8bit chars in the body, the body is\nencoded in iso-8859-1 as expected.\nMarked as verified.",
            "creator": "ji@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-15T19:14:59Z"
        }
    ]
}

{
    "bug_id": 8131,
    "summary": "Behaviour of image maps with broken images (i.e. AREA and alt text)",
    "comments": [
        {
            "text": "(This bug imported from BugSplat, Netscape's internal bugsystem.  It\nwas known there as bug #122145\nhttp://scopus.netscape.com/bugsplat/show_bug.cgi?id=122145\nImported into Bugzilla on 06/14/99 15:38)\n\nSome shapes do not reflect alternative text - specifically circles and\nsometimes, the polygon shapes.\n\n1. Go to: http://slip/projects/marvin/bugs/imagemap/imagemap_bug.html.\n2. Mouse over each of the shapes to see the alternative text.\n\nCannot get the alternative text to show when I mouse over circles. For the\nyellow polygons, I can get the alternative text if I mouse over the top point,\nbut it is inconsistent. Also inconsistent are the two large brown polygons.\nAlternative text for the blue squares is no problem.",
            "creator": "petitta@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1998-05-27T14:00:00Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "setting TFV to 5.0 per dp\nsetting owner to Pam per dp\nsetting Christine to QA Assigned to per me",
            "creator": "rubydoo123@aol.com",
            "timestamp": "1998-08-05T18:33:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 122141 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "christinehoff4@aol.com",
            "timestamp": "1998-09-11T01:20:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 322992 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "christinehoff4@aol.com",
            "timestamp": "1998-09-11T01:21:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Additional comments from duplicate bug #322992:\n\nWin 95 has this problem as well. In UNIX, there is no alternative text using the\nURL: http://slip/projects/marvin/bugs/imagemap/imagemap_bug.html",
            "creator": "christinehoff4@aol.com",
            "timestamp": "1998-09-11T01:26:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Revised initial comments to simplify explanation. As state, you can use:\n\n'http://slip/projects/marvin/bugs/imagemap/imagemap_bug.html' as the bug example\n\n\n\n------- Additional Comments From elig  Jun-04-1999 11:44 -------\n\n[QA Assigning to self for verification in 5.0, upon fixing of 1358.\n\nMoving to Bugzilla because I'll never remember to check it again if it's left in\nBugsplat.]",
            "creator": "christinehoff4@aol.com",
            "timestamp": "1998-09-17T18:10:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "kipp:\nWho handles image maps? I see your name in the cvs log\non nsImageMap.cpp so I thought I'd start with you.\n-p",
            "creator": "pnunn@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-21T19:24:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Umm, I tried it in apprunner and it works great. I also tried it in viewer and\nit works great...where's the bug?",
            "creator": "kipp@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-22T00:25:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I wrote this up over a year ago, when gromit was still in our vocabulary. Forgot\n\nthe bug even existed, shame on me!!!  Anyway, Eli if you would be so kind as to\n\ntake a look at this and close upon confirmation, I would be greatful....",
            "creator": "petitta@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-22T06:02:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Sure; the bug is my \"Jun-04-1999\" comment --- namely, that I was importing it as\na reminder to myself to confirm that it was definitively fixed.\n\nProbably should have marked it as RESOLVED/FIXED from the start. ;) Will verify\nthis within 2 business days.",
            "creator": "elig@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-22T17:25:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "After reading through 13.6 of the HTML 4 spec, I'm really not sure how to\nproceed:\n\n\t* The behavior that the bug report specifies --- that ALT tags should appear\nupon mousing over images --- is not specified (as far as I can tell) within the\nHTML 4 spec, and thus, is not supported by Seamonkey, as far as I'm aware.\n\nThe following test cases, I think, would cover the specification:\n\n\t* Configure the user agent to not display images, in which case the ALT text\nstrings should appear in the image map in lieu of the image.\n\n\tHowever, there's no way to do this, so I can't. ;)\n\n\t* Delete the image referenced by the image map. (\"User agents and authors\nshouuld offer textual alternates to graphical image maps for cases when graphics\nare not available or the user cannot access them.\" p. 165)\n\n\tHowever, if I do this, the ALT text doesn't appear as I expect it to --- it\njust displays the image file name.\n\n---\n\n\tThere's only one thing a respectable tester can do. Unveiling the magic lamp\nstowed away in the xHead pit, I doth summon the venerable ALT Text genie himself,\nIan Hickson.\n\n\tSo, Ian, I'm not sure how many wishes are left, but if you have any words of\nwisdom, they'd be most appreciated. ;) Thank you!",
            "creator": "elig@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-22T23:29:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "[and, yes, I'll attach the test case ASAP.]",
            "creator": "elig@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-22T23:40:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Created attachment 2375\nTest Case (1 of 2)",
            "creator": "elig@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-22T23:48:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Created attachment 2376\nTest Case (2 of 2)",
            "creator": "elig@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-22T23:51:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "elig: The magic lamp has been upgraded, nowadays sumoning it results in the QA\ncontact being replaced. :-O\n\nWe shouldn't be using 'alt' for the tooltips, we should be using 'title' for\nthe tooltips. But the alt text (possibly the <AREA>s') should indeed be used\nwhen the image of a client side image map is not available. NOTE: This is\nlikely to be not very easy to implement, I expect at some point in the near\nfuture we will see troy marking this REMIND...\n\n[updating summary as per bug description]",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-22T23:54:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Ok. Our current behaviour is unacceptable, since it results in the links being\nunusable. (IE5's behaviour is equally bad: the links are usable, but absolutely\nno feedback is given as to where the links are, or what they represent, since\nno alternative to image is provided if the image is not shown.)\n\nWhat we should be doing is making a 'menu' from the <area> elements, if the map\ncontains any <area> elements, or displaying inert text otherwise. I will draw\nup a more detailed behaviour spec in the next few minutes. I have also\nrequested a spec clarification from the authors of HTML4.01 since it is not\nclear what should happen when the MAP contains AREA elements _and_ normal A\nelements with coordinates.\n\nSome other image map bugs: bug 1427, bug 7304, bug 10545, bug 12721, bug 9140,\nbug 1882, bug 8027.\n\nI am setting up some dependencies and expanding the scope of the bug summary.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-23T01:03:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Here is a first draft of the possible behaviours that we should be exhibiting\nwhen trying to render an IMG element marked USEMAP for which we cannot actually\nrender the image (e.g. because images are disabled).\n\n=======================================================================\n\n1a. If the MAP element contains AREA elements, then:\n\n   Option 1: If dynamic transforms are not beyond the layout team:\n\n      1. Act as if the AREA elements found in the IMG's linked MAP were [also]\n         children of the IMG element, and apply the following styles:\n\n            IMG { display: block; } /* or inline-block */\n            IMG AREA { display: list-item; }\n            IMG AREA:after { content: attr(alt); }\n\n      2. Make sure AREA elements are recognised as links (just like A,\n         LINK, and XLinks).\n\n\n   Option 2: If dynamic context menus are not beyond the UI team:\n\n      1. Replace the IMG by its alt text, and style it as if it was a\n         link (this already happens, except for the underlining).\n\n      2. When someone clicks on the IMG alt text, generate a menu from\n         the 'alt' texts of all the AREA elements of that IMG's MAP.\n         Display the menu.\n\n      3. When someone selects one of the menu items, go to the URI\n         selected by the relevant AREA's href attribute.\n\n\n   Option 3: If intelligent behaviour must wait until the 5.1 release:\n\n      1. DO NOT replace the IMG with it's alt text. Display, instead,\n         the placeholder box with the broken object glyph and the alt\n         text inside the box.\n\n      2. Act as if that was the real IMG, thus giving the placeholder\n         box the hotspots that the <MAP> element specifies.\n\n     Note that this option causes accessibility problems and is\n     non-spec compliant.\n\n\n1b. Else: If the MAP element does not contain any AREA elements, then:\n\n   1. Act as if the IMG element was not marked USEMAP.\n      (i.e., display the alt text, but do not style it blue with a\n      hand cursor.) This probably requires that bug 11011 be fixed.\n\n=======================================================================\n\nOk, which of the options for (1) are we going to implement?",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-23T06:11:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Here's one for you to look at; I'd just later it, but we probably need to do\nsomething better for failed image maps.",
            "creator": "kipp@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-25T21:32:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Note. I'm currently in talks with the HTML4.01 editors to find out what we\nshould do. The discussion is being cc'ed to the www-html forum if you wish\nto read it. The thread in the archives starts at:\n   http://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/www-html/1999Oct/0113.html\nI suggest not doing anything until this is sorted out.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-25T22:29:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Accepting bug...",
            "creator": "nisheeth_mozilla@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-26T03:27:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Ok, talk with the html4 spec people has concluded with this behaviour spec:\n   http://www.bath.ac.uk/%7Epy8ieh/internet/discussion/usemap-alt.txt\n\nIs there any way for the layout engine to style elements in ways that the CSS\nselector syntax cannot do? According to the behaviour spec, we need to apply\ndifferent styles to AREA elements if the IMG element is broken and the MAP\nelement only contains AREA elements. Is this possible?",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-18T18:47:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I'm not sure whether this is possible or not.  Ccing Troy and Pierre.  Please\ntake a look at Ian's comment (11/18/99 10:47) on this bug report and answer it.\nThanks.",
            "creator": "nisheeth_mozilla@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-19T23:45:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "It's not possible to do that within the Style System but maybe in Layout, so I'll\nlet Troy answer.",
            "creator": "pierre@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-20T00:59:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "As usual Ian did a fine job of describing the landscape. At this point given how\nwe needed to be done last week option #3 seems the most sensible to. It should\nbe a simple matter of changing the image frame code so that if it is an image\nmap that can't be loaded we just display the broken icon and don't try to\ndisplay the alternate content",
            "creator": "troy@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-20T01:26:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Agreed. Whoever implements this temporary fix should resolve this bug as LATER\nand we can look at it again for 5.1.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-20T01:46:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 28822 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-23T09:07:31Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I have the temporary fix in my tree...  Will check in once the tree is open and \ngreen.",
            "creator": "nisheeth_mozilla@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-03-31T01:16:21Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "The fix is checked in.",
            "creator": "nisheeth_mozilla@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-04-01T00:10:15Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Actually, I wanted to later this one.   Re-opening and latering.",
            "creator": "nisheeth_mozilla@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-04-01T00:11:39Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Latering...",
            "creator": "nisheeth_mozilla@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-04-01T00:12:00Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "VERIFIED that our current behaviour is 4xP but suboptimal and that we will\nrevisit this after FCS. A specification of better behaviour is at \n   http://www.bath.ac.uk/%7Epy8ieh/internet/discussion/usemap-alt.txt\n",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-04-09T17:50:16Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Reopening and moving to Future...",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-05T19:15:16Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "...so that it appears on my radar.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-05T19:17:02Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "erm, _really_ reopening...",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2000-06-05T19:17:45Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "If I understand you correctly, Mozilla will deliberately _not_ show ALT text as\na tooltip, not for imagemaps, not for ordinary images, not anywhere else.  The\nonly use of ALT text is when the image itself is not displayed.  Can you confirm\nthat this is your official policy?\n\nAlthough tooltippifying ALT text may not be part of the W3C's recommendations,\nwouldn't it be worth keeping for compatibility with existing browsers?  I don't\nthink any web page author is going to complain that you're displaying the extra\ninformation to the user, and I don't think users are going to complain either. \nIt looks to me like a useful addition to the browser, especially since many more\npages have ALT than have TITLE.",
            "creator": "ed@membled.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-05-09T13:09:48Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "It's bad to show ALT text in tooltips because that encourages page authors to\nput text in ALT attributes because they want it to appear in a tooltip.  Such\ntext generally makes very bad alternate text for people who don't/can't load\nimages.  Encoraging authors to create such text hurts accessibility of the web.",
            "creator": "dbaron@dbaron.org",
            "timestamp": "2001-05-09T13:21:04Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "removing myself from the cc list",
            "creator": "rubydoo123@aol.com",
            "timestamp": "2002-03-08T20:06:07Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "changing component to layout",
            "creator": "pavlov@pavlov.net",
            "timestamp": "2002-04-25T23:44:15Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "->Image: Layout",
            "creator": "choess@stwing.upenn.edu",
            "timestamp": "2003-07-18T00:48:54Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Created attachment 133411\nAnother Test Case\n\nJust another test case.\n\nSuppose that \"images/links1.png\" has 300x50 pixels dimensions and\n\"images/links2.png\" has 200x50 pixels dimensions.",
            "creator": "stanio@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "2003-10-16T13:32:10Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "(In reply to comment #35)\n> It's bad to show ALT text in tooltips because that encourages page authors to\n> put text in ALT attributes because they want it to appear in a tooltip.  Such\n> text generally makes very bad alternate text for people who don't/can't load\n> images.  Encoraging authors to create such text hurts accessibility of the web.\n\nI don't agree with your opinion, since using \"alt\" is sometimes a great\nenhancement for authors ie to clarify what the image map is. Look at\nhttp://arbetarteater.nu > click on \"Våra Teatrar\" and you find a map over sweden\nwith small red dots, those dots are clickable links and contains a alt text that\nexplains what city the dot leads to. This is just one example of how to use the\nalt attribute. It works in IE6.0\nRegards\nDennis",
            "creator": "swedsi@netscape.net",
            "timestamp": "2004-10-03T09:11:28Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "(In reply to comment #40)\n> I don't agree with your opinion, since using \"alt\" is sometimes a great\n> enhancement for authors ie to clarify what the image map is. Look at\n> http://arbetarteater.nu > click on \"Våra Teatrar\" and you find a map over\n> sweden11\n> with small red dots, those dots are clickable links and contains a alt text\n> that\n> explains what city the dot leads to. This is just one example of how to use\n> the\n> alt attribute. It works in IE6.0\n\nMan, you're falling from Mars. Just read:\n\n\"Guidelines on alt texts in img elements\"\n<http://www.cs.tut.fi/~jkorpela/html/alt.html>\n\nmore precisely:\n\nhttp://www.cs.tut.fi/~jkorpela/html/alt.html#tooltip\n\nYou could also read the spec:\n\nhttp://www.w3.org/TR/html4/struct/global.html#title",
            "creator": "stanio@yahoo.com",
            "timestamp": "2004-10-04T17:17:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 321621 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "bugzilla@gtalbot.org",
            "timestamp": "2006-01-14T02:02:51Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 332458 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "jruderman@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2006-04-02T08:49:54Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Heh, I see this bug still hasn't been fixed with Gecko.\n\nWow, filed in the Netscape 5 days.\n\nBroken imagemap images still just display the broken image glyph.  I'm quite surprised this one hasn't been fixed actually.  It seems quite trivial to replace the imagemap image with a bulletpoint list of AREA links, using the IMG's ALT text as a title if specified.",
            "creator": "bugzilla@game-point.net",
            "timestamp": "2006-08-07T14:58:10Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 391376 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "crazy-daniel@gmx.de",
            "timestamp": "2008-12-24T22:16:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This might be possible now with the broken-image psuedoclasses.",
            "creator": "roc@ocallahan.org",
            "timestamp": "2008-12-28T10:25:31Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Possibly, yes.  At the moment, we never replace an imagemapped image with alt text, because doing that would make the imagemap not work.  We could create alt content that contains <a> nodes, I guess; the hard part is getting it to respond to mutations on the <map>.",
            "creator": "bzbarsky@mit.edu",
            "timestamp": "2008-12-28T17:20:47Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Since Firefox 4, this has detorated, see this test case from bug 592538 (it is a object element image map):\n\nhttps://bug592538.bugzilla.mozilla.org/attachment.cgi?id=595945\n\nIn Firefox 3.6, the <area> element are visible, via styling that bears some similarity to that which Hixie suggested in Comment 16. But now, since Firefox 4, the <area> element is impossible to style: Its computed style is {display:none}, whatever one does.",
            "creator": "xn--mlform-iua@xn--mlform-iua.no",
            "timestamp": "2012-02-10T04:10:47Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "The severity field for this bug is relatively low, S3. However, the bug has 4 duplicates and 14 votes.\n:emilio, could you consider increasing the bug severity?\n\nFor more information, please visit [auto_nag documentation](https://wiki.mozilla.org/Release_Management/autonag#severity_underestimated.py).",
            "creator": "release-mgmt-account-bot@mozilla.tld",
            "timestamp": "2022-10-17T12:14:28Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "imagemaps these days are probably not a priority to be more than an s3.",
            "creator": "emilio@crisal.io",
            "timestamp": "2022-10-17T12:50:58Z"
        }
    ]
}

{
    "bug_id": 8427,
    "summary": "[dogfood] sched: [Beta] make inter-application copy/paste work with Japanese characters",
    "comments": [
        {
            "text": "Tested 6-17-08 Win32 and Mac build.\n\nStep of reproduce\n1. Open the above URL page\n2. Select some Japanese text\n3. Select menu Edit | Copy\n4. Open new Notepad then, paste the Japanese characters\n\nNotice that Japanese characters you copied from the Browser is displayed \"..........\"",
            "creator": "teruko@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-17T21:42:17Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Per ftang, I reassign this to tague@netscape.com",
            "creator": "teruko@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-17T22:04:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "reassigning to cut and paste/clipboard owner on windows",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-17T22:21:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "tague is going to have to step through with the debugger to see what is going\non. The place to start is in nsPresShell.cpp in the DoCopy method.\n\nI think the problem is with the XIF converter. The selected content gets\nconverted into XIF. buffer.ToNewCString is passing the XIF encoded content into\nthe transferable.\n\nSo the question is:\n\nDoes XIF deal with double-byte chars?\nThe XIF converter was written by the editor group (Greg K.)\n\nAkkana do you know much about this?",
            "creator": "rods@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-17T23:00:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "My understanding is that XIF is supposed to work with double byte characters,\nbut I don't really know much about how that works.  You might check the file\n/tmp/xif.html on Unix or c:\\\\temp\\\\xif.html on windows (these are supposed to be\nconverted into standard temp file formats, and right now there's no equivalent\non mac) for the XIF file, for debugging purposes.  Maybe this will at least help\npin down where the problem is happening.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-17T23:45:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "The first question to answer is:\nDoes XIF really support double byte characters?",
            "creator": "rods@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-18T18:21:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I Akkana and Tague to figure out on the Japaneese platform if XIF is supporting\ndouble-byte chars, I can't do anything more with this bug until then.",
            "creator": "rods@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-06-22T15:46:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Reassigning this to tague:\n\nI need tague to sit down with akanna (in the debugger) and see if the\nXIFConverter supports double-byte chars.",
            "creator": "rods@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-01T21:35:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "moving to M9",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-02T22:11:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "In the past Greg K discussed this with ftang, and the design was to support\nthis.  ftang is out on paternity leave for another week.",
            "creator": "bobj@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-03T01:19:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Changing the component from Internationalization to Editor.",
            "creator": "bobj@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-03T01:20:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Why editor?  The test case had nothing to do with the editor.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-06T17:08:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I moved it to Ender because I thought Ender owned copy&paste, XIF and all that.\nWhat component should be used?",
            "creator": "bobj@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-08T00:17:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "XIF will actually support double-byte characters, the problem is that\nnsPresentationShell.cpp::DoCopy line 1635 does a ToNewCString each time the data\nis transfered to the cliboard object.\n\nreassigning to layout since that's where the presentation shell lives.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-13T22:13:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Adding rods and pinkerton to cc list since they own clipboard.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-13T22:19:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 5939 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-13T22:27:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Now that Greg is gone, who owns XIF?",
            "creator": "troy@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-13T22:30:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I own XIF.  But this is a copy issue, not a XIF issue; XIF already handles\nmultibytes and charsets (at least, Greg wrote code to do that though I doubt\nit's been tested much).",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-13T22:35:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Just to repeat what I previously said, this is *NOT* a XIF issue.\n\nThe problem is with string handling in the presentation context.  The\npresentation context takes the nsString from the XIF converter and does a\nToNewCString on it.  It's the ToNewCString operation that is causing the data\nloss.\n\nThe XIF converter deals with double byte characters correctly.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-14T00:37:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Tague -- I accept your comments at the end of this bug on face value, but I'd be\ngreatful if you could send me a pointer as to where the ToCString bug that\nreferring to is. Thanks.",
            "creator": "rickg@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-17T12:30:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I changed the PresShell to get a unicode string instead of a cstring. I think\nthis is what is needed. I don't think he was trying to imply that there was a\nbug in nsString.",
            "creator": "rods@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-19T17:00:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Just to clarify some confusion, what rods said is correct.  The problem is not a\nbug in ToNewCString/ToCString but a bug resulting from using ToNewCString in\nthis context.  sorry for the confusion.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-19T19:11:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "reassigning to rods since the data loss problem has moved from nsPresShell.cpp\nto nsXIFFormatConverter.cpp.\n\n(a) Plain Text Conversion:\n  if (aToDataFlavor->Equals(kTextMime)) {\n    if (NS_OK == ConvertFromXIFToText(srcText, text)) {\n      *aToData = (void *)text.ToNewCString();\n      *aDataToLen = text.Length();\n    }\nConvertFromXIFToText does the appropriate conversions; however, the call\ntext.ToNewCString causes data loss for any non-ASCII data.  The Unicode data in\ntext needs to get converted to the system code page (windows), system script\n(Macintosh) before getting written to the clipboard.\n\n(b) HTML Conversion\n  } else if (aToDataFlavor->Equals(kHTMLMime)) {\n    if (NS_OK == ConvertFromXIFToHTML(srcText, text)) {\n      *aToData = (void *)text.ToNewCString();\n      *aDataToLen = text.Length();\n    }\nConvertFromXIFToHTML is not converting \"double-byte\" characters appropriately.\nIt needs to generate an HTML fragment which includes the encoding used for the\nHTML and it also needs to convert the HTML to a reasonable encoding which can\nexpress the data.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-19T23:13:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Adding Pav to cc list, since he's run into this problem working on the gtk\nclipboard.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-22T01:52:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Reassigning to Stuart, he has signed up to solve this problem",
            "creator": "rods@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-06T20:20:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "this has become a blocking problem.\n\nwe need this functionality so mail & news can test and debug Japanese mail\nsupport.  without cut and paste we have no way of providing them with test cases\nor helping them to debug problems that we find",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-06T20:23:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "There is *no* way I will get this fixed for M9.  I haven't even looked at the\ntransferable code yet and won't have a chance for a while.",
            "creator": "pavlov@pavlov.net",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-06T21:57:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Yes, that is ok. Move it to M10. I briefed tague on this earlier. He did express\na desired to have it fixed for M9, but understands the currect state of affairs.",
            "creator": "rods@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-06T22:01:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "it would be nice if we could at least fix this on windows so mail and news can\nmake better headway.\n\nPav :- this is still a blocker - it's preventing mail & news from doing testing\nand debugging of work we've requested.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-06T22:13:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "fixing it on windows is going to require me to fix it everywhere.  I don't have\ntime right now to work on it.",
            "creator": "pavlov@pavlov.net",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-06T22:22:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "rods made some changes today that fixed this problem for intra-application copy\nand paste. (thanks rod!)  this still needs to be fixed for inter-application cut\nand paste, but rod's changes do take care of our blocking problem.\n\ni'm changing the severity, but i still would like to see this worked on for M10.\n-------\nteruko - can you please do a quick QA on japanese cut and paste within\nseamonkey.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-18T18:54:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "need estimated remaining duration in status whiteboard",
            "creator": "trudelle@acm.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-18T23:15:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Pav and I looked at this a bit yesterday.  The nsHTMLToTXTSinkStream class does\na ToNewCString in the case where there's no unicode converter, which is the case\nfor ucs2 -- we have specific logic NOT to get a unicode converter for UCS2.\n\nPav has some other issues inside the clipboard code that he's working on, but\nmeanwhile I have two possible fixes for the html to txt converter which both\nseem to fix that part of the problem:\n\n1. Go ahead and create a unicode converter even for ucs2\n2. Continue not using a converter for ucs2, but in the case where we don't have\na converter, do everything in unicode rather than using ToNewCString().\n\nI can check in either or both of these fixes today (or whenever the tree opens\nnext), but I'd like some feedback from someone who understands this issue better\nas to which of these is appropriate.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-20T21:32:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "this is already assigned to me, i don't need to be on the cc list",
            "creator": "pavlov@pavlov.net",
            "timestamp": "1999-08-20T21:35:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "akkana do you want this bug since I think all of the current remaining stuff you\nwere working on?",
            "creator": "pavlov@pavlov.net",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-07T06:31:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "akkana has two different solutions but doesn't know which one is right.  could\nsomeone in i18n talk to her and find out which she should check in?",
            "creator": "pavlov@pavlov.net",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-08T23:59:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "if you have questions or need some advice about potential solutions, send me\nemail, call me or drop by my cube.  this is a core bug, the core team needs to\ndrive resolution of this.",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-09T19:14:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I don't understand Tague's comment.  Who are the \"core team\", and who on the\n\"core team\" is qualified to make decisions about which unicode converter is the\nright one to use?",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-09T19:18:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "let me clarify.  this bug is the responsibility of the people who own cut and\npaste -- this bug is not owned by the international group, so it should not be\nre-assigned to me.\n\nif you or pavlov or anyone else working on cut and paste need my help in\nresolving this bug, it is glady given but the owners of cut and paste need to\ncontinue to be responsible for driving this bug to completion.  if part of\ngetting this bug fixed requires consulting time from me, the owner of the bug\nneeds to take the initative to find me and ask the questions that he or shee\nhave not just reassign this bug to me and expect for it to be fixed.\n\n/t",
            "creator": "tague@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-09T19:25:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "As akkana stated on 08/20/99, she has two possible fixes.  If you want this\nfixed, then you need to advise her as she requested.  If you don't want to own\nthis bug, should I file another one saying that we are waiting on i18n to give\nus an answer?",
            "creator": "pavlov@pavlov.net",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-13T04:26:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 13677 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-13T23:50:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Update: the problems previously mentioned in nsHTMLToTXTSinkStream.cpp no longer\nexist and are not the culprit here.  Turns out that after calling the sink\nstream to convert the plaintext output to an nsString, the nsXIFFormatConverter,\nin the plaintext case, does a ToNewCString().\n\nThis may be indicative of a fundamental problem with the clipboard APIs -- the\nfact that Convert() takes char* arguments rather than nsString arguments.  But\nit seems to work okay for html copy/paste of Japanese characters (such as the\nones at the bottom of the html editor init page).",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-15T01:50:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Here are some results of the copy/paste with today's\nbuild (M10) using Japanese strings for copying.\n\nWhat works:\n\n1. copy/paste from Editor to Editor\n2. copy/paste from Editor to Mail compose body window\n3. copy/paste from Plain text editor to Editor\n4. copy/paste from plain text to Mail compose body window\n\nWhat does not work:\n\n5. any text into Plain Text editor window (We see dots instead)\n6. any text into Mail compose Subject field (We see dots instead)\n\nOn Browser window:\n\nCopy function does not seem to have been enabled.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-15T18:28:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Agreed, that's also what I see.  Any time the clipboard converts to plaintext,\nthe Japanese characters get lost.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-15T18:42:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "We need to keep in mind that in addition to these\nintra-application copy/paste, we also need to do a decent job\nof inter-application copy/paste for which the original bug report\nwas filed.\n\nAs of this buuild, we are not able to copy/paste properly from\nMozilla into applications such as NotePad, Communicator 4 Editor,\netc. On the other hand, IE5 can copy Japanese or Latin 1\nstrings into these applications though an application such\nas NotePad seems to be monolingual, i.e. cannot copy Latin 1\ncharacters with accents into a Japanese Notepad without\nlosing the accents. But IE5 can copy Japanese or Latin 1 into\nComm 4's Editor as long as the Editor's charset menu is changed\nto Japanese, Latin 1 or UTF-8.\n\nWhat IE5 sends to the Win clipboard, however, does not paste well into\nMozilla Editor. So we probably need to take care of this issue also.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-15T19:37:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Note that when I said \"clipboard\" I meant nsClipboard, which is used both for\ninter- and intra- application copy/paste.  Since the code in this class doesn't\nhandle Japanese, it will be broken for both cases.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-15T19:43:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I realize that inter-application copy/paste may not work 100%\neven under the best of circumstances but I would like to suggest\nthat we should agree on what should work. I think a good\nstart would be to say that what works for Communicator 4\nshoud at least work. (So, for example, IE5 to Mozilla Editor should\nfor non-ASCII strings.)",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-15T19:52:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "By the way when would nsClipboard code be able to handle\nJapanese (and other languages)? Is this on the schedule\nsomewhere?",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-15T19:54:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I can't speak for the xptoolkit people's schedule, but replacing that\nToNewCString line with something more like what the kHTMLMime clause is doing\nshouldn't be that huge a deal, I wouldn't think ...  it was probably just an\noversight.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-15T20:04:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "The clipboard code can handle one-byte (plain text) and two-byte (unicode) data\ndepending on the flavor used. If the \"text/plain\" flavor is used, you get one-\nbyte ascii with no encoding information (yet, tague said he'd do that). If you\ndon't use that flavor, all should be ok.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-15T22:49:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "What flavor should be used in order to get unicode plaintext?  Perhaps the\neditor should be looking for that as well as for kTextMime for text insertions.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-15T23:19:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "there is a kUnicodeMime type",
            "creator": "pavlov@pavlov.net",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-15T23:54:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I tried calling trans->AddDataFlavor(kUnicodeMime) just before calling\ntrans->AddDataFlavor(kTextMime) in nsHTMLEditor::Paste.  When I try to paste\ninto a plaintext editor, I crash in:\n\n#0  strcmp (p1=0x0, p2=0x40561dd6 \"text/plain\")\n    at ../sysdeps/generic/strcmp.c:38\n#1  0x40553f2a in nsBaseClipboard::CreateDataFromPrimitive (aFlavor=0x0,\n    aPrimitive=0x0, aDataBuff=0xbfffe5d4, aDataLen=1080677867)\n    at nsBaseClipboard.cpp:180\n#2  0x40537871 in nsClipboard::SelectionGetCB (widget=0x89add80,\n    aSelectionData=0xbfffe97c, aInfo=0, aTime=0) at nsClipboard.cpp:787",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-16T00:07:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "i assume that means the 1st param to strcmp is null so it crashes (gotta love\nthose c standard libraries). What is that param, and why is it null?",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-16T00:15:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "moving to m11",
            "creator": "trudelle@acm.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-24T04:56:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Not for dogfood as of PDT meeting.",
            "creator": "jevering@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-07T15:01:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "We should do when we past text to the native clipboard ?\non Window\n1. put Unicode to the native clipboard with CF_UNICODETEXT\nsee\nhttp://msdn.microsoft.com/library/sdkdoc/winbase/clipbrd_5nfo.htm\nhttp://msdn.microsoft.com/library/sdkdoc/winbase/dde_9f1u.htm\nhttp://msdn.microsoft.com/library/devprods/vs6/visualj/vjref/com.ms.wfc.app.Data\nFormats_f.html\nhttp://msdn.microsoft.com/library/devprods/vs6/vfoxpro/foxhelp/lnggetformat_meth\nod.htm\n\n2. Put Text in ACP (ANSI CodePage) with CF_TEXT to native clipboard\nIn order to do that, you first find the charset name by get the\nnsIPlatformCharset service. Call the GetCharset method with selector\nkPlatformCharsetSel_PlainTextInClipboard to get a charset name back. Then you\nget a nsIUnicodeEncoder for that charset. Then you convert the Unicode text by\nusing that conveter.\n\non Mac,\n1. put the Unicode to the native clipboard with 'utxt'. see\nhttp://developer.apple.com/techpubs/mac/TextEncodingCMgr/TECRefBook-154.html\nfor details.\n\n2. put the text in system script to the native clipboard with 'TEXT', same as\nwindow, use the nsIPlatformCharset service....\n\non Linux-\n1. convet to text in locale encoding by using the same way which got\nnsIPlatformCharset first...\n\nThis IS a dogfood for IQA since they need to create test cases Japanese text in\ndaily bases. And if this is not implement, they they cannot switch to use\nSeaMonkey as their daily browser to do their work.",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-07T20:04:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Adding dogfood again; Copy and paste are in the dogfood list for English, right?\nSame for Japanese then. Please, explain what the reasons were to remove the\ndogfood criteria.",
            "creator": "msanz@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-07T20:26:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Putting on PDT+ radar.",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-13T00:43:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "mass-moving all m13 bugs to m14",
            "creator": "trudelle@acm.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-02T23:00:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "How can a PDT+ bug be an M14 bug? This has to be fixed by Dogfood.",
            "creator": "msanz@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-02T23:12:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "sorry, I was trying to not move any PDT+ bugs, must have overlooked this one.\nHow did it get to M13 anyway? moving to M12",
            "creator": "trudelle@acm.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-04T01:58:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Pav, since you are currently 'most doomed', could someone else (Pink, RodS) take\nthis bug?",
            "creator": "trudelle@acm.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-17T03:41:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "yes",
            "creator": "pavlov@pavlov.net",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-17T03:43:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "(pink reads over the torrid history of this bug)\n\n\n\ngod, i guess i'll take it off of pav's hands. it will take me some time to come\n\nup to speed on the i18n issues and the correct solutions on the individual\n\nplatforms. don't expect this one fixed by m12.\n\n\n\nmoving to m13, accepting bug.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-17T03:48:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Pav recently (last week) fixed the gtk clipboard so that it handles Unicode\npasting, and Naoki and I made corresponding changes to the editor to use unicode\nin preference to text.  Has anyone checked this week to see if this bug is still\na problem?",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-17T18:15:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "we don't copy and paste from other apps properly as far as i know",
            "creator": "pavlov@pavlov.net",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-17T18:44:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "It cannot be  moved to M13; this is a Dogfood PDT+ bug that affects\ninternational and it should be fixed by M12. Peter, please review target\nmilestone.",
            "creator": "msanz@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-18T21:14:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "We are aware of the bug's importance, and have triaged it accordingly, relative\nto our other priority work.  Pink, could you please add the target fix Friday\ndate to the summary whiteboard?",
            "creator": "trudelle@acm.org",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-18T22:00:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "annotating tfd 12/3.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-18T22:16:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This bug has been metamorphosizing through the days and months of discussion.\nIt is not very clear now  just what we are supposed to be accomplishing.\nbobj, msanz, teruko, and I met and discussed this bug, and then teruko and\nI separately to agree onthe current facts.\n\nWhat we decided to do is to reduce the scope of this bug to just intra-application copy/paste\nwith Japanese and other non-ASCII characters.\nteruko will file a new bug which will address inter-application copy/paste issues -- which might not be\na Dogfood item but certainly part of Beta 1Intl requirements.\n\nHere are the current state of affairs with Mac, Win32 and LinuxM12  builds of 11/18/99\nwith regard to copy/pasting ** within Mozilla **. There may be a few minor differences among the\nplatforms but there seem to be pretty good cross-platform consistency in what is and what is\nnot working.\n\n1. Copy/paste from Edit field/area (Ender) into another Edit field/area (Ender) is now working\n   for non-ASCII characters. (There may be a few minor bugs left  but these should be addressed in\n   other new bugs.)\n2. Copy/paster from non-Edit filed, e.g. Browser page and Mail message viewing window, into\n   an Edit filed/area is NOT working. This function is disabled and not available through any means.\n\nWe consider #2 to be a Dogfood item. International testing involves using data from many different\nlanguages which we may not be able to input easily through keyboards. Without copy/pasting working\nfrom a Browser page or Mail Viewing Window, our daily work is greatly hampered and in some cases\nsuch as Thai and Vietnamese, just totally blocked.\n\nThe requirement of this bug then is to make item #2 working.\nCurrently copy/cut menu or keyboard actions are totally disabled and not available.\nThis must be made to work. There already may be a bug on this elsewhere calling for\nthe copy/cut function to be enabled on the Browser and Mail Viewing windows. If so,\nmake this bug dependent on it. However, this bug should NOT be made a duplicate of it since\nwe don't know if non-ASCII copy/pasting will work once the function is enabled from\nthese windows. The reqruirement for us is that A) the function be enabled from these\nnon-Ender areas both from a menu and via keyboard shortcuts, AND  b) such operation must\nbe successfully applicable to non-ASCII data including Japanese and Latin 1.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-19T08:08:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "There is not \"cut\" from Brower or Mail Veiwing windows -- so please disregard the\ninclusion of \"cut\" in my remarks above.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-19T08:18:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "the fact that copy/paste are grey'd out is a different bug.  that is an XP apps\nbug.  this bug is meant for making sure that we can copy and paste i18n text to\nand from other applications.",
            "creator": "pavlov@pavlov.net",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-19T09:31:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Thanks. There seem to be a number of bugs open right now for Copy/paste in various components\nof Mozilla including one for Browser (Bug 14026), MessengerCompose (Bug 5063), etc. I don't see\none specifically for Messenger Viewing window, though.\n\nI'm afraid that we need to hunt down all of them and make sure that non-ASCII\ncopy/paste works when they are fixed.\n\nI changed the summary to reflect the fact that this bug is targeted to 'inter-application i18n concerns'.\nSorry for the confusion.\nDo we need a summary of the current state of affairs for inter-application copy/paste?",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-19T10:19:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Ok, well if we're waiting on #2 (which is out of my hands), then this is\ndependant on xpapps hooking up cut/copy/paste. Right now, that work (enabling the\nedit menu items for the content area) is not on the beta feature list, according\nto don, but maybe someone (davidm) can hook it up when he fixes 14026.\n\nI'll talk to davidm today and see if we can get that fixed and on his radar to\ndo. I will leave this M13 since we don't have a timeframe for when this\nfunctionality will be testable.\n\nThe fact that it works w/in editor gives me the warm fuzzies, however.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-19T19:42:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "FYI: Bug 14025 is the bug on CCP being disabled in the browser window.  See the\ndependency tree for that bug for other bugs.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-19T19:45:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Oops, that should have been 14026.  I see that's already in this bug's\ndependency tree.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-19T19:49:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "So, pinkerton, you probably can remove the PDT+ since the remainings of the bug\nare not dogfood. Thanks.",
            "creator": "msanz@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-19T22:35:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "removing from pdt list, per talking with msanz. Yay! ;)",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-19T22:46:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "PDT- given comments at bottom of document.",
            "creator": "rickg@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-19T23:48:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I created a separate bug 21414 for XIF to kTextMime conversion.",
            "creator": "nhottanscp@yahoo.co.jp",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-10T21:56:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Reassigning to momoi for verification if this is still a problem now that one can\ncopy text out of the browser.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-07T19:13:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "reassign back to pinkterton.\nPinkerton, please do not assign this bug to QA engineer ( momoi, momoi and\nteruko are QA engineer. They won't FIX this bug. ) If you think you fix it, mark\nit fix and IQA will pick them up and verify them. If you are not sure about your\nfix. Send them mail in private or put your comment in bug report. Thanks.",
            "creator": "ftang@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-07T19:21:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I thought the \"for verifcation\" part of Pinkerton's comment was pretty clear...",
            "creator": "pavlov@pavlov.net",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-07T19:23:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Okay, fine. marking fixed. we'll play that game if you want.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-07T19:27:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Can you comment on to what extent we fixed this bug?\n\nI'm less concerned about copying from external application to\nMozilla --> we should ask for Unicode or native clipboard, right?\n\nIt is the exporting of data that concerns me. If an external\napplication does not ask for Unicode data from the clipboard, is\nis supposed to work?\n\nFor example, copying JPN data from Mozilla to Win Notepad works but\nit does not work to WordPad and a few non-MS applications I tried\nwith 1/6/00 Win32 build.\n\nBy the way, for clarification, I believe it is legitimate to\nassign a bug to a QA when a developer wants to know the current\nstatus of the problem so that he/she can guage how the matter\nstands at present without first marking it fixed.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-07T19:43:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "It surprises me that it works in NotePad (which only understands text/plain) and\ndoesn't work in WordPad, which understands unicode. Did you mix up the names by\naccident? This is opposite from what I expected to hear.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-07T21:42:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "No, I described it right the first time. To elaborate,\n\nThis is WinNT4 only. On Japanese NT4,\n\nNotepad.exe --> display JPN data correctly from Mozilla\nwrite.exe (Wordpad) --> displays JPN data from Mozilla incorrecty as dots\n                         rather than as characters.\n\nIt is NotePad which is OK. Both are version 4.0 which get updated to\nwith Service Pack 5.\n\nWe really need to look at Win98/95 where there might, potentially, more\nproblems than one would encounter on NT4.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-07T21:54:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Sounds like we're advertising plaintext ahead of unicode in the lists of flavors\nwe support.  Either that, or wordpad is asking for plaintext when it could be\nasking for unicode, in which case it would be a wordpad bug.  Does wordpad\ndefinitely accept Japanese text from other applications (to rule out that\npossibility), applications which can also paste ascii?",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-07T22:00:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Yes, it does.\nFor example, we can copy JPN data from Notepad to Wordpad, and also\nfrom another JPN text editor (e.g. Hidemaru), which has the same problem\naccepting Mozilla JPN data, to Wordpad.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-07T22:06:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Here's what I've found:\n\nWordPad: asks for text/plain\nNotePad: asks for text/unicode\n\nI think the reason that WordPad doesn't work is that we don't have text/plain in\nthe correct charset right now. I imagine (and Naoki can tell me if i'm right)\nthat his changes that he submitted to me for charset conversion will fix this\nproblem.\n\nIs this a correct assessment? Is there a bug filed on that yet? If so, then it\nshould be a dependency of this bug. The fact that unicode copy/paste works gives\nme the warm fuzzies that we don't have any fundamental problems.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-08T00:23:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Yes, my change for 21414 is for pasting text/plain. I need a code review for\nthat.\nBTW, my change does not change pasting to mozilla. Is that working now?",
            "creator": "nhottanscp@yahoo.co.jp",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-08T00:29:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "but that bug has to do with text/mime, not text/plain. Am i just crazy? I'll shut\nup if i am.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-08T00:31:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "21414 is for kTextMime which is text/plain (see nsITransferable.idl).",
            "creator": "nhottanscp@yahoo.co.jp",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-08T00:37:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "my god, you're right. doh!",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-08T00:39:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "As expected, on Windows 98-Japanese the results were much\nworse. Here's the current status with Mozilla (1/6/99 Win32 build)\nand WritePad, NotePad and another application. Unlike on NT4-J,\nhere WritePad performs slightly better. The following involves\ncopying a Japanese string.\n\n1. Copy from Notepad/Hidemaru Editor & paste to Mozilla -- Fail\n    (looks like ASCII garbage)\n2. Copy from WritePad & paste to Mozilla -- Pass\n3. Copy from Mozilla to Notepad/Hidemaru Editor -- Fail (dots in place of\n    characters)\n4. Copy from Mozilla to WritePad -- Fail (dots in place of characters)\n\nI'll attach an .jpg image illustrating the above results\nusing Mozilla composer. You can see what they look like. Note\nthat there is only 1 Pass out of 4 cases under Win98-J.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-08T07:16:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Created attachment 4068\nJPEG image of the Win98-J copy/paste results",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-08T07:18:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I should add that there are applications like Communicator 4.x,\nOutlook Express 4/5 and Internet Explorer 4/5 which can easily do\ncopy/paste to/from Mozilla without any corruptions on Windows 98-J.\nBut by and large, the number of such applications is small. So\na well-known application like PageMill-Japanese edition has problems\nsimilar to NotePad.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-08T07:42:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Hopefully nhotta's/my fix for text/plain and correct charsets will help those\nother apps that don't understand the CF_UNICODETEXT flavor. Try these tests again\non Monday and let me know.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-08T22:34:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "i was wrong about 21414 being fixed. i misapplied nhotta's patch. more work needs\nto be done there, but it shouldn't be that hard. anyway, QA should ignore my\nstatements that all this should work on monday ;)",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-10T19:05:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "ok, 21414 is fixed (the right way this time). hopefully this should improve for\nthe 1/11 build. can someone test for me then?",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-10T23:27:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "i had a flash last night watching tv. I think my fix for 21414 broke unicode\ncopy/paste for non-roman languages. sigh. i know how to fix it, so don't be\nsurprised if it regresses for a few days.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-11T19:07:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "removing target fix date (heh) and pdt- for reclassification, now that we're off\nto beta and not m12.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-13T01:43:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "As you say, non-ASCII copy/paste seems to be generally broken now, even the intra-application\ntype.",
            "creator": "momoi@alumni.iu.edu",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-13T01:51:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Oh yes, it certainly is. I did a number to it. Fixes are all in my tree just\nwaiting for the tree to go green and we should be back to \"normal\" but charset\nstuff should work with plain text.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-13T01:55:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Putting on PDT+ radar.",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-14T00:12:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Can someone test this now and see if it is any better/worse wrt the i18n aspects.\nI checked in my fixes so all should be back to normal.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-14T00:49:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Adding 24010 to the dependency (copy from other app to mozila).\nI think copy to other app is now fixed (21414).",
            "creator": "nhottanscp@yahoo.co.jp",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-15T00:26:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "moving to m14 as the new dependency was just added.",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-15T00:34:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Added beta1 in Keywords.",
            "creator": "teruko@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-01T23:16:38Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "with 24010 fixed, no more work should be needed on this. marking fixed.\n",
            "creator": "mikepinkerton@mac.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-01T23:19:03Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I tested this in 20000208 Win32 and 2000020410 Mac.\n\n   Copy/Paste Japanese\n       Editor (Mozilla) / Editor (Mozilla)   OK\n                             / Mail (Mozilla)      OK\n                             / Plain text editor(Mozilla)  OK\n                             / Text area in Browser (Mozilla) OK\n                             / Notepad             OK\n                             / Wordpad             OK\n                             / Composer (Communicator 4.x)   OK\n\n       Notepad          / Editor (Mozilla)   OK\n                             / Mail (Mozilla)      OK\n                             / Plain text editor(Mozilla)  OK\n                             / Text area (Mozilla) OK \n                            \n      WordPad           / Editor (Mozilla)   OK\n                             / Mail (Mozilla)      OK\n                             / Plain text editor(Mozilla)  OK\n                             / Text area (Mozilla) OK \n\n  Copying Browser and test area does not work completely.  And, I need to investigate the copying and pasting in Linux how\n  it will work.   ",
            "creator": "teruko@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-08T23:22:52Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Added that work is being done to verify in Status Summary.",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-09T01:02:57Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "After I spent time on investigating Japanese Copy/Paste in linux, I do not think we have the right environment in Linux.\nWe are using Redhad 6.0 US plus pseude Japanese locale.  The other editer cannot handle Japanese characters.\n\nI will verify this bug since this works in Mac, Win32 build.  \n",
            "creator": "teruko@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-10T00:14:51Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "vi doesn't work if started in Japanese locale?\nWhat about using kterm?",
            "creator": "bobj@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-10T00:30:06Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Copy/paste between kterm and mozilla doesn't work either. 4.x on linux  also has this problem.",
            "creator": "ji@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-10T00:48:53Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I think copy/paste in Linux has problem.  After the beta1, we will investigate more.\n",
            "creator": "teruko@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-10T01:03:42Z"
        }
    ]
}

{
    "bug_id": 8705,
    "summary": "[FEATURE] Better status/progress feedback",
    "comments": [
        {
            "text": "Must defer till M9; this really needs to await Necko since it will be\nimposing substantive changes in this area.",
            "creator": "law@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-03T03:27:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Move this to M10.",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-31T00:11:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "is there any status/progress on this?",
            "creator": "cmaximus@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-09-30T18:24:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Gagan has got notifications flowing so the status bar is back to where it was\npre-Necko.  Still need to enhance the progress meter.  May be getting a\nvolunteer from outside.",
            "creator": "law@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-10-20T20:10:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Putting on PDT+/Dogfood radar.",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-02T01:45:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Bill says he may be able to bring this in to 11/26.",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-11T02:52:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Changing to PDT- per discussion of PDT long poles.  The most we could rate as\ndogfood is an indication that some progress is taking place.  It is not\nnecessary to see better percentage values etc. for dogfood.",
            "creator": "jar@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-17T21:54:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "OK, moving out to M13.",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-11-17T23:26:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Move to M14.",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-23T03:42:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Ooops.  Should be M13.",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "1999-12-23T06:30:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 22973 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-04T18:19:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "spam: added self to cc list.",
            "creator": "bugzilla@iwaruna.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-04T21:01:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Sorry to be clueless, but does this bug deal with progress bar and status in\nsave as dialogues or the page loading status bar at the bottom of browser\nwindow, or both.",
            "creator": "paulmac@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-07T08:09:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This bug refers to the progress meter at the bottom of the browser window.  It\nis supposed to switch to thermometer mode when loading big/slow pages (and the\nstatus bar should say something like \"3% of 47K at 3.2K/sec\").",
            "creator": "law@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-07T17:53:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Good, then I'm in the right place. I was trying to verify bug 16386, which deals\nwith a trivial spelling error in 'transferring data' that used to appear there,\nbut I don't see that anymore. Is that related to this?",
            "creator": "paulmac@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-07T18:08:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Added dependency on bug #13374, Travis' webshell work.",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-14T00:35:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Moving out to M14 as the requisite changes to the webshell and uri loader won't\nhappen till post-M13.",
            "creator": "law@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-01-14T00:42:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 20972 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-01T20:36:12Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Adding this from bug #20972:\n\n1. The progress bar message does not change at all when a new page is loaded.\nDuring loading, it shows the \"Document: Done (xx seconds)\" message of the\nprevious page loaded; instead it should display \"Contacting server\", \"Waiting\nfor reply\", etc.\n\n2. If a page load is interrupted, the progress message shows \"Document: DOne (xx\nseconds)\" where xx seconds is the time between clicking on a link and clicking\non the stop button.  This is slightly confusing, since whatever is shown in\ntheprogress bar should apply to the page currently viewed.",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-01T20:36:55Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Just FYI, bug #18724 handles mostly this same thing. Should this depend on\nit?",
            "creator": "tomi.leppikangas@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-24T15:38:18Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Move to M16 ...\n",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-04-06T21:10:48Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Putting on [nsbeta2-] radar. Not critical to beta2.",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-05-12T18:13:47Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Move to M20 target milestone.\n",
            "creator": "don@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-05-25T23:13:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "whoops, almost thought this had to do with the downloading status dialog.\nchatted with claudius, who clarified things (yay) --back to him. :-)",
            "creator": "bugzilla@iwaruna.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-11T20:12:35Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Nav triage team: [nsbeta3+]; look at Don's 2/01 comment",
            "creator": "verah@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-25T20:25:02Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Adding nsbeta3 keyword to bugs which already have nsbeta3 status markings so \nthe queries don't get all screwed up.",
            "creator": "dveditz@mozilla.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-08-09T20:04:31Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I'm going to resolve this FIXED.  Progress/status messages are pretty good \nnowadays.  One sees (typically):\n\nResolving host <host>\nConnected to <host>\nReading <url>\nDocument: Done\n\nPlus, a percentage read, when applicable.\n\nThere might still be some flakiness, but those should be addressed via separate \nbugs (which might already exist, for all I know); those bugs aren't likely to be \nnsbeta3'ers.",
            "creator": "law@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-08-10T00:34:19Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "marking VERIFIED Fixed with 2000082108 builds (note there exist other, documented bugs)\n",
            "creator": "cmaximus@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-08-23T00:45:33Z"
        }
    ]
}

{
    "bug_id": 9589,
    "summary": "InstallObject::toString() should return PRUnichar*",
    "comments": [
        {
            "text": "toString() returns a char* -- should be PRUnichar*\n\nNeed to check other API's as well for char*",
            "creator": "dveditz@mozilla.com",
            "timestamp": "1999-07-10T13:35:26Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "checked in fixes to return PRUnichar* in all ::toString() functions\n\nfixes applied to the following C++ objects:\nnsInstallDelete\nnsInstallExecute\nnsInstallFile\nnsInstallFileOpItem\nnsInstallPatch\nnsInstallUninstall\nnsWinProfileItem\nnsWinRegItem",
            "creator": "cathleennscp@netscape.net",
