{
    "bug_id": 27771,
    "summary": "page up/down when focus is in text field should scroll containing page",
    "comments": [
        {
            "text": "Click in a text field, then hit page up/down. Since text fields don't know how\nto scroll, the event should bubble up to the page and be handled there, but\ninstead, nothing happens. (4.x scrolls in this case.)\n\nI notice that htmlBindings.xml has page up/down bindings for text fields. It\nshouldn't, and they should be removed, but removing them doesn't fix this bug.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-15T01:07:20Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Depends on 24645\n",
            "creator": "saari@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-02-15T01:42:33Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "As a note to this. If the text box contains more text than can be contained,\npage up/down seems to \"attempt\" to scroll but actually doesn't. (It flickers up\nbut then jumps down again).\n",
            "creator": "b.judd@xtra.co.nz",
            "timestamp": "2000-03-09T00:59:12Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "> As a note to this. If the text box contains more text than can be contained,\n\narrgh\n\nfor that second \"contained\" read that as \"shown\". sorry\n",
            "creator": "b.judd@xtra.co.nz",
            "timestamp": "2000-03-09T01:01:29Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Single line text fields do not respond to page up/down on 4.x for Win or IE.\nMultiline text fields have another bug describing the flickering attempt to scroll.\n\nMoving to M20\n",
            "creator": "saari@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-03-21T01:55:00Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This is a major usability loss compared to 4.x, since I have to use twice as\nmany mouse clicks to navigate around in a bugzilla page, and twice as many\ntransitions from keyboard to mouse.  Nominating for beta2 consideration since if\nI didn't work here, this would stop me from switching to mozilla.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-03-21T01:59:35Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Is this a loss on unix? It isn't on Windows.\n",
            "creator": "saari@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-03-21T02:41:54Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Yes, in 4.x on Unix, when focus is in a text field, page up/down will still\nscroll the containing page.  I didn't know until just now that Windows didn't\nbehave that way (wow, Windows users put up with a lot! :-)\n\nShouldn't our event model just handle this magically if it were working right?",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-03-21T02:56:30Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Yeah it should... I think... Well, I know for sure that the XBL bindings for \npage-up and page-down are getting called (change their commands to be arrow left \nor right and you can remap page up/down). The question then becomes why the \ncommand dispatcher doesn't seem to be executing those. \n\nOk, I'll pull this back to M15 then and figure out what dimesion those commands \nget lost in.\n",
            "creator": "saari@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-03-21T03:40:29Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "setting priority\n",
            "creator": "saari@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-03-21T05:21:06Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Mass-moving most M15 bugs to M16",
            "creator": "trudelle@acm.org",
            "timestamp": "2000-04-05T23:39:58Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 35325 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "brade@comcast.net",
            "timestamp": "2000-04-11T20:58:16Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Putting on [nsbeta2-] radar.",
            "creator": "leger@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-05-02T00:27:35Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Mass-moving nsbeta2- bugs to M20",
            "creator": "trudelle@acm.org",
            "timestamp": "2000-05-25T22:47:22Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This is still a big functionality loss compared to 4.x (have to do a lot more\nclicking to navigate pages like bugzilla).  Adding 4xp keyword and nominating\nfor nsbeta3.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-20T00:24:29Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Mass update:  changing qacontact to ckritzer@netscape.com\n",
            "creator": "ckritzer@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-27T21:44:53Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "no time for this, nsbeta3-/future/helpwanted",
            "creator": "trudelle@acm.org",
            "timestamp": "2000-07-28T01:02:18Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Updating QA Contact.\n",
            "creator": "bugzillaabuse@lorcas.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-09-25T19:39:42Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Anyone feel like this warrants an RTM honorable mention? -d\n",
            "creator": "bugzillaabuse@lorcas.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-10-03T01:38:01Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Target Mozilla 1.0",
            "creator": "saari@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-10-26T23:29:33Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "pgup/pgdn in a textbox probably needs to unfocus the textbox as well as \nscrolling the page down, so that if the user presses an arrow key later, the \npage doesn't scroll back to the textbox.",
            "creator": "jruderman@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2000-11-16T05:05:16Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "mozilla 0.9",
            "creator": "saari@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2000-12-20T02:18:06Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Any reason this bug is marked as linux-only?",
            "creator": "jruderman@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-03T07:34:02Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Perhaps because nobody has bothered to see if it exists on other platforms\n(although I would assume it does)?  Have you?",
            "creator": "dbaron@dbaron.org",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-03T15:43:08Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I don't know about others, but when I report a bug, unless I have proof it\naffects other OS's I set OS to what I tested it on.  I suspect others do the\nsame.  There's no \"unknown\" in OS, nor is it a multiselect.\n\nIn this case, I suspect that, plus the fact that this feature was in 4.x NS for\nUnix but not in 4.x NS for win32 caused it to be listed as Linux.  It affects\nall OS's, though, so OS -> ALL.\n",
            "creator": "rjesup@jesup.org",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-03T15:47:10Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Saari's first comment asserted that mac and windows apps don't normally scroll\nthe containing page like Unix apps do if the focus is in a text field.  I don't\nknow if any other windows/mac people have looked at the bug, nobody else seems\nto have expressed an opinion.  Seems like it would be useful functionality for\neverybody, but that's a Unix person speaking. :-)",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-03T20:09:40Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Well, I just checked IE5 on mac, and it does in fact scroll the page. So I dunno\nwhat the *right* thing is, but I'm marking it as all platforms for now.\n\nOnce the text field is scrolled off the page I can type in it and it doesn't\nscroll back into view. So much for usable UI. Is it supposed to scroll back into\nview on unix?",
            "creator": "saari@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-03T22:36:49Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Just checked Linux 4.7: it doesn't scroll back into view, and typed text does\ncontinue to go into the off-screen text field.\n",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-03T23:28:13Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "That just doesn't seem like good UI to me, but whatever. ",
            "creator": "saari@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-03T23:34:47Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This is 4xp on Mac, too. For scrolling back into view when you type, see bug \n64170.\n",
            "creator": "mpt@postinbox.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-11T11:02:23Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 57961 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "mpt@postinbox.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-11T11:02:47Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Bug 64170 is for scrolling within the textbox, not for scrolling the page to \nmake the textbox visible.  (If you file a new bug for that, please cross-\nreference it with 64170.)  I still think pgup/pgdn in a single-line textbox \nshould blur the textbox in addition to scrolling the page, though.",
            "creator": "jruderman@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-11T14:46:33Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "It looks like there are differing opinions on what the Page up and Page down\nbuttons should do.\n\nBehavior 1,  when the Focus is any where but a multiline or scrolling text box\nthe keys move the view of the page up or down one view full. When it is in a\nmultiline or scrolling text box the PageUp or PageDown keys move view of the\ntext in the box up or down one view full.\n\nBehavior 2, The Page Up and Pge Down always move the web page view up or down\none view full.\n\nThe question now is which one is right So we can fix this bug properly.\nRegardeless I think agree that it shouldn't do nothing and it shouldn't flicker\nthe text.\n\nMy two cents are that page up and down be used for moving the web page view for\ntwo reasons, there are many instances of this being usefull and there are\nrelatively few large test fields that would require scrolling that could not be\neasily handled by the up and down arrows.",
            "creator": "lake@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-12T01:05:01Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "There could be some other complicating issues, like IFRAMEs (which is the page?).\n\nEven with those complications, I think it could be boiled down to 2\npossibilities (plus some ugly ones in the middle):\n\n 2) outermost scrollbar -- in other words, scroll the containing page rather\n    than the IFRAME, and the page rather than the TEXTAREA\n\n 1) innermost scrollbar -- the reverse\n\nThen there's the somewhat ugly issue of what to do if a scrollbar is not\npresent -- do we do nothing, or do we skip to the next one in/out?  In\nother words, if we choose (2) and the user is doing message composition in\na webmail program where there is a large scrolling textarea occuping most\nof a non-scrolling page, should PgDn scroll the textarea?  Moving to the\nnext scrollbar in/out would be inconsistent, but sometimes useful.  I'm\nnot sure which should win.\n\nBut that's a digression, since I think the real point of this bug is that we\nought to do something like (1) or (2) or something in between, rather than\ndoing nothing.",
            "creator": "dbaron@dbaron.org",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-12T01:18:49Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I guess I'd expect the text area, rather than the containing page, to scroll if\nthe focus was there, but could live with either solution.\n\nThe important thing is that the containing page should scroll when focus is in a\ntext field (single line, non scrollable).\n\nPersonally, I'd prefer that it didn't blur the text field -- I do sometimes\nscroll down to check background of the bug then go back to typing -- but that's\na minor issue.  If you don't like being able to type when the widget is off the\nscreen, maintaining focus and auto-scrolling on typing would be a better solution.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-12T01:56:14Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "ms intellimouse scrolling does innermost scrollable object.\nie style 1\ndo we skip to the next one in/out? it does\n\ndo i like their approach? yes.",
            "creator": "timeless@bemail.org",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-12T06:36:18Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "> My two cents are that page up and down be used for moving the web page view for\n> two reasons, there are many instances of this being usefull and there are\n> relatively few large test fields that would require scrolling that could not be\n> easily handled by the up and down arrows.\n\nAn obvious counterexample is message composition fields in Webmail accounts -- \nsuch messages may be very long, so Page Up and Page Down would be quite useful \nfor scrolling through them. For that reason I vote for Behavior 1. This would \nalso provide consistent behavior between IFRAMEs and TEXTAREAs.\n",
            "creator": "mpt@postinbox.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-12T07:57:06Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "A pref panel would solve this problem-but then we'd have to do it both ways.  The \nthing is that there are two types of users: those who would use this, and those \nwho won't:).  \n\nIf we have to choose one, I'd say that maybe 20% of users use their browsers \nprimarily for e-mail.  The rest don't.  While I see the merit of using focusable \niframes as far as PgUp/PgDn are concerned, they are not the majority of users by \na long shot (I think, since I have no hard evidence to back this statement).\n",
            "creator": "bugzillaabuse@lorcas.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-12T08:35:42Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "My 2 cents: page up/down should scroll the item with focus if it's scrollable.\nIf it's not scrollable, pop out one level, wash, rinse, and repeat.  Simple,\nmakes sense to people, doesn't surprise people.\n\nSo, if it's in a scrolling text field, it will scroll it.  If the text field\nisn't scrollable, it will scroll the window (or frame/whatever) the text field\nis in.\n\nAs for how to deal with a multiline text field that doesn't have a scrollbar -\nit's not scrollable.  So page up/down would scroll the window.  (this is more\narguable).\n\nThis is the behavior of most wheel-mouse implementations I think.\n",
            "creator": "rjesup@jesup.org",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-12T15:54:48Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Speaking of the mousewheel, mousewheel should also scroll the page when focus is\nin a text field (or also, if possible,  in a textarea that doesn't have a\nscrollbar).  Should that be a separate bug (or is it already?)  I long for this\nseveral times a day.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-27T02:11:37Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Bug 62431 covers mousewheel scrolling needing to get the parent.  The fix may be\nthe same for both, though -- bryner suggests that\nnsGfxTextControlFrame2::GetScrollableView should return the parent if the text\nconrol is not scrollable, which sounds very sensible, and I'm going to play with\nthat and see if it fixes the problem.",
            "creator": "akkzilla@shallowsky.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-27T03:11:13Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Fix for 62431 didn't fix this; GetScrollableView() is never called on page \nup/down when in textfields.",
            "creator": "bugzilla@blakeross.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-27T06:11:30Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Reassigning QA Contact for all open and unverified bugs previously under Lorca's\ncare to Gerardo as per phone conversation this morning.",
            "creator": "ian@hixie.ch",
            "timestamp": "2001-01-29T18:21:46Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "QA contact updated",
            "creator": "madhur@formerly-netscape.com.tld",
            "timestamp": "2001-06-15T22:13:46Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 86765 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "bugzilla@iwaruna.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-06-20T00:06:21Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I have made a suggestion in bug 29086 that implies that Mozilla should use the \nfirst version of the semantics suggested by David Baron (Scroll the outermost \niframe eg containing page), but use the scroll lock key to change to the second \nsemantic (Scroll the innermost frame capable of responding to the page up/page \ndown request).\n\nJust thought I'd direct interested people to that bug for my full suggestion.",
            "creator": "andrewdoull@hotmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2001-11-14T00:17:17Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Hello,\n\n  My 2 cents, I think that anything that don't have scroll bar shouldn't keep\nthe scroll events for themselves.  \n  \n  Radio Buttons, Text Field, CheckBox, Image, etc shouldn't keep the scroll\nevents but bubble them to their container.  So, focus on a text field shouldn't\nprevent scrolling the whole page.\n\nThanks\n",
            "creator": "daniel.savard@gespro.com",
            "timestamp": "2002-01-24T17:51:41Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 170364 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "brade@comcast.net",
            "timestamp": "2002-09-24T21:39:22Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 171936 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "mjl+bmo@michaellefevre.com",
            "timestamp": "2002-12-05T17:39:17Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Resetting target from 1.0.1\n\nI'm willing to do this if someone would point me in the right direction.",
            "creator": "rjesup@jesup.org",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-05T16:24:32Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Note: this is (already) an accessibility bug; people who don't have or can't\neasily use mice would find usage easier if page up/down bubbled.",
            "creator": "rjesup@jesup.org",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-05T16:39:17Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Created attachment 113617\nPatch for 1-line input widgets\n\nThis is a patch that solves the problem for 1-line text input widgets.\tIt's a\nbit kludgy (though nsFocusController already is incestuous with the different\ntext input elements), and doesn't handle multi-lines (which is more fun).\n\nThe \"right\" solution is to change the interfaces so that a command can return\nthat it didn't really want to be run after all, and let the search continue. \nThis would require significant changes to GetControllerForCommand() and the\ncallers of the commands.",
            "creator": "rjesup@jesup.org",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-05T20:56:04Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Created attachment 113629\nUpdated patch\n\nI originally did the patch against an older version.  Updated patch for current\ntrunk",
            "creator": "rjesup@jesup.org",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-05T21:55:06Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "This to the best of my knowledge so far is how this bug should be fixed:\n\n1. In nsEditorController.cpp create a new method that only registers those\ncommands needed for <input>.\n\n2. In nsEditorRegistration.cpp register a new component that calls that method\ninstead of the existing method.\n\n3. In nsHTMLInputElement.cpp create an instance of the new component instead of\nthe existing component.",
            "creator": "neil@httl.net",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-06T14:54:35Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "So, armed with additional knowledge (thanks brade!) the problem is this:\nplatformHTMLBindings translates PageDown for browser and textarea, not input\nhowever the browser translation is intercepted by input anyway...\nSo, the fix now appears to be to search for the controller starting with the\nelement on which the key was mapped to a command.",
            "creator": "neil@httl.net",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-06T15:47:23Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "The code for that belongs in nsXBLPrototypeHandler.cpp;\ndelete lines 263-294 (and the else on line 295),\nand fix the method currently at line 547\n(and rename it to GetControllerForCommand for consistency).\nIt should work like the same method in nsFocusController.cpp,\nbut starting at the event receiver, not the focused element.",
            "creator": "neil@httl.net",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-06T16:02:45Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "How do we deal with the more general case here?  Pagedown in a multi-line\ntextarea?  That requires the command handler to be able to say \"sorry, no\nthanks, give someone else this command\".\n\nYou could have page up/down bubble out if there are no scrollbars, or you could\nfollow CUA, and have them do beginning/end, and only bubble out if you're\nalready on the first or last line.\n\nNote: I don't want to discourage anyone from solving this for the \"simple\" case\nof single-line widgets first.\n",
            "creator": "rjesup@jesup.org",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-06T18:54:17Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Be very careful. When the focus is in the mailnews quick search field, you don't\nwant the delete key to say \"nothing to delete here, try bubbling up\".",
            "creator": "neil@httl.net",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-06T22:09:48Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I think we need 2 things here:\n1. Commands on the editor controller to handle page up/page down, that do the\n   right thing for single-line inputs, and scrollable ones.\n2. Some way that a command can return NS_ERROR_COMMAND_NOT_HANDLED, and have\n   the command dispatch system look for another controller that will handle it,\n   or have the controller call IsCommandEnabled() (which I think it does already),\n   but be willing to look up the controller chain if necessary.",
            "creator": "sfraser_bugs@smfr.org",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-11T20:50:13Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Having keys start applying to a different widget without a change in focus seems\nlike bad UI in general.  Do we really need a mechanism to implement that\nbehavior?  I expect that if PgDn does something, I can hold it down for a bit,\nand when it stops doing what it's doing it won't start doing something else\n(like scrolling the page instead of the textarea).\n\nOr am I misunderstanding what you're trying to do?",
            "creator": "dbaron@dbaron.org",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-11T20:58:53Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "dbaron: that would be affected by the implmentation of the commmand.\nNS_ERROR_COMMAND_NOT_HANDLED should only be returned when it's OK to dispatch\nthe command to another controller (i.e. only for non-scrollable things). If a\nscrollable thing is scrolled to the end, the command should just return NS_OK.\n\nThis does highlight a difference between using a NS_ERROR_COMMAND_NOT_HANDLED\nreturn value, and using IsCommandEnabled to implement command \"bubbling\". The\nlatter would cause the kind of behaviour that dbaron describes, so is probably\nthe wrong thing to do.",
            "creator": "sfraser_bugs@smfr.org",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-11T21:04:39Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "For the record, I still dispute the proposed fix by Simon (comment 58 and\ncomment 60).  While it might be a nice thing to have in the long run, I don't\nthink it solves the root of this bug.  In my opinion, the problem in this bug is\nthat the keybinding found a controller to handle the command but then didn't\ndispatch it to the controller that wanted to handle it (it sent it based on focus).\n\nAn alternative, hacky solution to this issue would be to rename the commands so\nthat no controllers use the same command strings (kind of ugly and undesirable\nbut it would fix this issue).",
            "creator": "brade@comcast.net",
            "timestamp": "2003-02-12T18:10:31Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I'm just a new Firebird user.  I'm seeing something I think is like this in\nFirebird 0.7.  Sometimes when I go to a text page the keyboard page up/down keys\ndon't seem to do anything.  If I try the cursor arrow keys somewhen along the\nvarious key presses trying to move things, then the page up/down seems to work\n(focus shifted?).  But they don't always work.  Sometimes pressing once gives me\na page down, but pressing again produces no movement.  I noticed this especially\ntrying to read a news story from a \"My Yahoo\" page.",
            "creator": "paulgrogers@lycos.com",
            "timestamp": "2003-10-20T17:47:13Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Is my problem related to this?\n\nI'm using Thunderbird Trunk nightly.  Every day I paste a piece of text/HTML into an email.  The cursor is there at the end of what I have pasted, and I can type or backspace just fine.  But when I press CTRL-Home to go up to the top, it doesn't work until I use the mouse to click somewhere in the window.  I'm pretty sure this\nis a new problem in the last month or two.",
            "creator": "peter.reynolds@googlemail.com",
            "timestamp": "2007-11-15T09:03:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 557766 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "jo.hermans@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2010-04-08T20:17:08Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "The behavior should be the one expected by the user, not the one imposed by the browser. So, it should probably be configurable.\n\nThis also concerns the arrow keys and the Home and End keys (not only page up/down), and even when a multi-line text field has the focus, it may be a good idea to scroll the page on up/down arrow keys (see example below).\n\nI don't think that single-line fields and multi-line fields should behave differently (though this could be configurable), because these cursor keys are also used for the history in single-line fields.\n\nNow, to give an exemple, some web sites automatically gives the focus to a text field on load, e.g. allocine.fr (single-line field, used for searching) and identi.ca (multi-line field, used to compose a message). In these two cases, the text field is initially empty (so that moving the caret up or down doesn't make sense ­— for single-line fields, browsing the history can make sense but not necessarily useful in this context). Moreover it is not immediately clear for the user that the text field has the focus, in particular because:\n  * the focus hasn't been set explicitly by the user, but automatically by the web site;\n  * Firefox doesn't show when the page has the focus and when it doesn't;\n  * the text field with the focus may not be visible, even on load.\nSo, the user wonders why scrolling with the keyboard doesn't work, and even when one knows the web site, this behavior is quite annoying.\n\nI think the behavior could be the following: By default, the cursor keys would have their normal effect for a text field that has the focus. Then there would be (configurable) exceptions (I don't like exceptions, but I think that here this is unavoidable for usability reasons). For instance, when a field is empty and/or when the focus has not been explicitly given by the user. Another one could be whether the cursor is at the end for the corresponding movement.",
            "creator": "vincent-moz@vinc17.net",
            "timestamp": "2011-08-06T15:13:30Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 701616 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "john.p.baker@bristol.ac.uk",
            "timestamp": "2011-11-11T09:18:42Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 675486 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "MatsPalmgren_bugz@use.startmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2012-10-24T16:09:53Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 1122720 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "gijskruitbosch+bugs@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2015-01-16T23:51:20Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "(In reply to Jesse Ruderman from comment #20)\n\n> pgup/pgdn in a textbox probably needs to unfocus the textbox as well as \n> scrolling the page down\n\nNo. Not on Mac, anyway. On Mac, Page Up and Page Down just move the view. :-) (Window$ does horrible things.)",
            "creator": "nicolas.barbulesco@supinfo.com",
            "timestamp": "2015-04-20T15:01:26Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Decreasing the priority as no update for the last 2 years on this bug.\nSee https://github.com/mozilla/bug-handling/blob/master/policy/triage-bugzilla.md#how-do-you-triage \nabout the priority meaning.",
            "creator": "sledru@mozilla.com",
            "timestamp": "2018-10-23T16:23:02Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Hello 21-yo thread! Please fix this problem. It is crucial to my comfort to have this function on websites. I need the \"Page Down\" button to actually scroll the page when text field is focused. Please, I don't want to go back to Chrome for this function.",
            "creator": "nvlquack@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2020-06-01T15:21:57Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "(In reply to Randell Jesup [:jesup] (needinfo me) from comment #38)\n> My 2 cents: page up/down should scroll the item with focus if it's\n> scrollable.\n> If it's not scrollable, pop out one level, wash, rinse, and repeat.  Simple,\n> makes sense to people, doesn't surprise people.\n\n\nI think I'd prefer this version of the expected behaviour. However, maybe one clarification is to scroll the item with focus if it's \"scrollable in the direction\". That means if you are scrolling down in a textarea, and you are at the end of the textarea, we will pop out of the textarea to start scrolling the parent. \n\nThis closely follows the expected behaviour when using the mousewheel to scroll and the cursor is hovering over a textarea: When the textarea is scrollable in the desired direction, scroll the textarea. If not, scroll the parent. \n\nThis preserves the expected user behaviour of allowing scrolling inside the focused element while also allowing scrolling the parent as well.",
            "creator": "gliu10000@gmail.com",
            "timestamp": "2024-04-09T18:27:18Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Hi James,\nIt looks to me that this bug and bug 1239595 are duplicates. I am flagging you here because I am not quite sure if/how my action of setting duplication causes unexpected tracking consequences for the Web Compatibility:Knowledge Base bugs.  These two platform bugs block different Knowledge Base bugs.",
            "creator": "htsai@mozilla.com",
            "timestamp": "2024-05-16T09:01:28Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "If they're dupes the right thing do do is to dupe the core bugs, dupe the knowledge base bugs, and ensure that the remaining kb bug is updated to block all the known site reports, and maybe add some text to the kb bug about the additional symptoms to look for. Unfortunately we don't yet have tooling to handle this automatically.",
            "creator": "james@hoppipolla.co.uk",
            "timestamp": "2024-05-16T09:15:31Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 1239595 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "james@hoppipolla.co.uk",
            "timestamp": "2024-05-16T10:58:16Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Hi Jamie and Masayuki,\nWe had discussions in bug 1806931 comment 14 which I believe is relevant to this issue. Apparently there are webcompat interests here, while there are also a11y concerns. Is there a compromised-but-acceptable solution to both webcompat and a11y? Bug 1806931 comment 14 seems to indicate no, but I wanted to ensure we're all aligned with the conclusion and the next steps. Thanks.",
            "creator": "htsai@mozilla.com",
            "timestamp": "2024-09-04T08:59:34Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Well, we could redirect the commands to `nsISelectionController` for the page [here](https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/rev/5959ec6b84d66592a77a3e5e2d2aedc1b3e7d4c5/editor/libeditor/EditorCommands.cpp#680,750-751). So, technically, it's fixable without big patches.\n\nOn the other hand, I'm still worry about new complaints about the new/Chrome behavior. I think that we need scenarios that when users feel inconvenient with current behavior to solve the a11y concerns.",
            "creator": "masayuki@d-toybox.com",
            "timestamp": "2024-09-05T00:02:53Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I agree that the concerns are contradictory and I haven't come up with any compromise solution. That said, regarding web compat, as I understand it, [Chromium have acknowledged that they consider the current Chromium behaviour to be a bug](https://issues.chromium.org/issues/41417806#comment7). If they were to fix this, that would partially resolve the web compat issue here in a way which is better for accessibility. Of course, it's difficult to know if/when this is likely to get attention from them.",
            "creator": "jteh@mozilla.com",
            "timestamp": "2024-09-05T02:32:04Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "It might be a solution to fix the Chromium behavior by us.",
            "creator": "masayuki@d-toybox.com",
            "timestamp": "2024-09-05T04:30:36Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "(In reply to James Teh [:Jamie] from comment #78)\n> I agree that the concerns are contradictory and I haven't come up with any compromise solution. That said, regarding web compat, as I understand it, [Chromium have acknowledged that they consider the current Chromium behaviour to be a bug](https://issues.chromium.org/issues/41417806#comment7). If they were to fix this, that would partially resolve the web compat issue here in a way which is better for accessibility. Of course, it's difficult to know if/when this is likely to get attention from them.\n\nGood reminder! We're going to take this to our contact on the Chromium team.",
            "creator": "htsai@mozilla.com",
            "timestamp": "2024-09-05T09:14:07Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "If I understand correctly, the [Chromium bug](https://issues.chromium.org/issues/41417806) is about a textarea (e.g. in the bug report: \"Type some text on the textarea\"), while the Mozilla bug is about single-line text fields (in the [bug description](#c0): \"Since text fields don't know how to scroll\", which is not true for textareas).",
            "creator": "vincent-moz@vinc17.net",
            "timestamp": "2024-09-05T14:09:12Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "(In reply to Vincent Lefevre from comment #81)\n> If I understand correctly, the [Chromium bug](https://issues.chromium.org/issues/41417806) is about a textarea (e.g. in the bug report: \"Type some text on the textarea\"), while the Mozilla bug is about single-line text fields (in the [bug description](#c0): \"Since text fields don't know how to scroll\", which is not true for textareas).\n\nThat's true. However, as I noted in bug 1806931 comment 13:\n\n> [Not consuming page up /down is] perhaps reasonable for single line inputs, which is the case here. I'd argue it isn't reasonable for multi-line inputs, though, and contentEditables can't specify whether they're multi-line or not in a standard way as far as I know, so this also applies there.\n\nAlso, in bug 1806931 comment 14, :masayuki noted:\n\n> Even a single line input may have big height like a multi-line input. Therefore, I think it could make users confused too.",
            "creator": "jteh@mozilla.com",
            "timestamp": "2024-09-05T23:24:28Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "There are pages that put the focus in a text field by default. So the current behavior is rather bad. Or perhaps there should be an option to control the behavior.",
            "creator": "vincent-moz@vinc17.net",
            "timestamp": "2024-09-05T23:59:42Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I don't think it's constructive to argue about this at length :), but to provide an alternative perspective, it's debatable whether the browser's behaviour is bad or whether the site is behaving badly. Arguably, the browser is doing exactly what it's supposed to do: when focus is in a text field, cursor navigation and scrolling should be restricted to the text field. If the intention is that the page should be immediately scrollable by the user, the page shouldn't set focus to a text field in the first place.\n\nAn option is one possibility, but this is obscure enough that I suspect most users won't know what the option means or won't know to look for it.",
            "creator": "jteh@mozilla.com",
            "timestamp": "2024-09-06T00:03:35Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "(In reply to James Teh [:Jamie] from comment #84)\n> An option is one possibility, but this is obscure enough that I suspect most users won't know what the option means or won't know to look for it.\n\nWhen affected by an issue or wanting a different behavior, users often search the web (now, there's also AI) and will find the answer if the option is present.\n\nBTW, Firefox could also also detect that the [Page Up] / [Page Down] keys are used in a single-line text input while they have no effect and propose the option as a tip.",
            "creator": "vincent-moz@vinc17.net",
            "timestamp": "2024-09-11T10:05:29Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "So, as the patch author from 22 years ago... I don't think my patch will apply anymore.  :-)  I regret not pushing people to do something, however - we could have had a partial fix 22 years ago.   However, what my patch tried to do could still be valid (fix this for the 1-line text input case).  It really sounds like Chrome isn't likely to change their behavior anytime soon.  I don't think our users will get that annoyed if we fix the one-line text case.\n\nmasayuki - you indicated it can be fixed for the simple case fairly easily, correct?",
            "creator": "rjesup@jesup.org",
            "timestamp": "2024-09-28T18:53:30Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Yeah, I think so. `sInputHandlers` does not register the `PageUp` and `PageDown` as page scroll keys. So, it can have them, and `SelectionMoveCommands` can redirect the command to `PresShell`.",
            "creator": "masayuki@d-toybox.com",
            "timestamp": "2024-09-30T06:27:26Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "(In reply to Masayuki Nakano [:masayuki] (he/him)(JST, +0900) from comment #87)\n> Yeah, I think so. `sInputHandlers` does not register the `PageUp` and `PageDown` as page scroll keys. So, it can have them, and `SelectionMoveCommands` can redirect the command to `PresShell`.\n\nHey Masayuki and here,\nTrying to push for a clear next step here. Am I right that we can go implement the 1-line text input case without waiting on [the Chrome bug](https://issues.chromium.org/issues/41417806) to be fixed? Or should I mark this bug as webcompat:blocked instead?",
            "creator": "htsai@mozilla.com",
            "timestamp": "2024-12-11T10:40:59Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "I meant that it's easy to change our behavior for aligning to Chrome, but Jamie and I (and also the Chrome's bug reporter) don't think that Chrome's behavior is reasonable. So, I think Chrome developers should change their behavior unless they believe their behavior is better.",
            "creator": "masayuki@d-toybox.com",
            "timestamp": "2024-12-12T02:31:03Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "Created attachment 9473799\ntestcase 1\n\nHere's a testcase, for convenience with testing this issue, and to help with reasoning about how our behavior currently differs from other browsers (and how this issue differs from e.g. bug 1239595 which was brought up as a possible-dupe but I think is in fact quite different).\n\nSTR for this testcase:\n1. Scroll down a bit and click to focus the text input or the textarea.\n2. Press your PageDown key repeatedly.\n\nEXPECTED RESULTS:\nAfter one or more PageDown keypresses (enough to reach the end of the text control), any subsequent PageDown keypresses should cause the top-level document to be scrolled.  (In particular: when no more scrolling/caret-moving can be done inside the text control, then additional PageDown keypresses should be targeted at the nearest containing scrollport; along the lines of jesup's intuition in comment 38.)  This is what happens for mousewheel scrolling, basically.\n\nACTUAL RESULTS:\nPageDown just stops having any effect once the caret reaches the end of the text control.\n\nFirefox gives ACTUAL RESULTS.\nChromium/WebKit give EXPECTED RESULTS.",
            "creator": "dholbert@mozilla.com",
            "timestamp": "2025-03-22T04:24:51Z"
        },
        {
            "text": "*** Bug 1886126 has been marked as a duplicate of this bug. ***",
            "creator": "james@hoppipolla.co.uk",
            "timestamp": "2025-09-03T17:12:52Z"
        }
    ]
}
